{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FXtAVH38WJc8vy57eXD1pG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: best\nnarrative_guidance: \"Architecture-specific vulnerability generalizes across DMC tasks — trained dt-embedding is a reliable CIVO trigger in imagination-based actor-critic regardless of environment dynamics. TAWM's MPC success vs our imagination failure creates a clean architectural distinction. Dual-pathway finding elevates contribution from 'one failure mode' to 'systematic fragility taxonomy'. Paper framing unchanged: strong diagnostic analysis with actionable architectural guidance.\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008d (trained dt_emb, collapse) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_011\"\n    description: \"Replicate CIVO collapse with 2 additional seeds on Walker Walk\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_peak: 4.12\n      seed1_nve_peak_step: 55000\n      seed1_eval_105K: 131\n      seed1_collapse: true\n      seed1_collapse_onset: 25000\n      seed2_nve_peak: 2.05\n      seed2_nve_peak_step: 25000\n      seed2_eval_105K: 268\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed2_self_correction: true\n      nve_above_1.5x_count: \"2/2\"\n      collapse_rate: \"1/2\"\n      cross_seed_nve_separation: 1.0\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires walker_additional_seeds_nve_separation=[1.0,1.0] — perfect 2/2 seeds exceeding nve>1.5x at 35K+. Collapse remains stochastic (1/2) because the feedback loop lock-in depends on early training stochasticity (initialization, replay buffer sampling order). Seed 1 sustains nve>1.5x across consecutive checkpoints (positive feedback locks), seed 2 transiently exceeds then self-corrects as actor gradient signal from real returns overwhelms overestimated critic. This 100% diagnostic marker + stochastic collapse pattern is the strongest quantitative separation for C2.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Walker Walk 008d multi-seed NVE trajectory\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"seed1_nve\", \"seed1_eval\", \"seed2_nve\", \"seed2_eval\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 1.78, 167, 2.05, 141]\n          - [35000, 1.71, 146, 1.24, 164]\n          - [45000, 2.09, 141, 1.62, 141]\n          - [55000, 4.12, 83, 1.41, 172]\n          - [65000, 1.23, 250, 0.94, 209]\n          - [75000, 1.48, 178, 0.66, 197]\n          - [85000, 1.15, 202, 0.51, 236]\n          - [95000, 1.73, 133, 0.43, 279]\n          - [105000, 1.57, 131, 0.38, 268]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"NVE time-series: seed 1 sustained above 1.5x dashed line (35K-55K) then partial recovery but re-collapse; seed 2 briefly touches 1.5x then monotonically declines. Shaded threshold region.\"\n    analysis: \"Perfect 2/2 diagnostic separation — both trained dt_emb seeds exceed nve>1.5x at 35K+. Collapse probability 1/2 is within expected range [0.33, 0.67]. The bifurcation between sustained vs transient overestimation at 35-55K is the critical window determining outcome.\"\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008f (dt_embed_dim=0, stable) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_012\"\n    description: \"Replicate causal control stability — no dt_embedding → no CIVO\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_peak: 1.02\n      seed1_nve_peak_step: 25000\n      seed1_eval_105K: 503\n      seed1_collapse: false\n      seed2_nve_peak: 1.26\n      seed2_nve_peak_step: 25000\n      seed2_eval_105K: 148\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      nve_above_1.5x_count: \"0/2\"\n      causal_direction_confirmed: true\n    result_rationale: \"Without dt_embedding conditioning, the value function cannot exploit precise dt predictions in imagination. The RSSM still produces dt>1 transitions in training data (subsample ON), but without the conditioning channel, the critic receives no signal to systematically overestimate dt>1 trajectories. Both seeds remain below nve=1.5x throughout, confirming dt_embedding is the necessary amplifier. Seed 2 shows declining eval after 75K due to the inherent difficulty of H=8 learning without dt-awareness for reward prediction (reward_MAE=0.747, 75x baseline), but this is performance limitation, not CIVO collapse.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Walker Walk 008f causal control NVE (both seeds)\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"seed1_nve\", \"seed1_eval\", \"seed2_nve\", \"seed2_eval\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 1.02, 197, 1.26, 169]\n          - [35000, 0.73, 207, 0.79, 179]\n          - [45000, 0.83, 194, 0.72, 211]\n          - [55000, 0.97, 173, 0.66, 239]\n          - [65000, 0.37, 375, 0.53, 244]\n          - [75000, 0.42, 324, 0.33, 359]\n          - [85000, 0.65, 246, 0.60, 227]\n          - [95000, 0.54, 319, 0.90, 191]\n          - [105000, 0.30, 503, 1.14, 148]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"NVE trajectory: both seeds confined below 1.5x threshold (dashed line never crossed). Contrast with 008d seeds where both cross 1.5x before 45K.\"\n    analysis: \"4/4 perfect separation maintained: trained dt_emb 2/2 exceed 1.5x, untrained/absent 0/2 exceed. 008f's performance variance across seeds (503 vs 148) reflects the cost of removing dt_embedding — reward prediction degradation limits learning stability — but the CIVO mechanism is completely blocked.\"\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 (dt=1 imagination) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_014\"\n    description: \"Multi-seed validation revealing dual-pathway vulnerability\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 870\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.80\n      seed1_pathway: \"healthy\"\n      seed2_eval_105K: 137\n      seed2_nve_105K: 3.78\n      seed2_pathway: \"B_policy_instability\"\n      seed2_entropy_direction: \"increasing (-5.21→-3.83)\"\n      collapse_rate: \"1/2\"\n      pathway_a_present: false\n      pathway_b_present: true\n      dual_pathway_confirmed: true\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires dual-pathway finding characterizable with distinct signatures. Seed 1 is healthy because dt=1 imagination eliminates Pathway A (CIVO requires dt>1 imagination for value overestimation feedback loop). Seed 2 collapses via Pathway B — the trained dt_embedding corrupts the critic's state representation even when imagination uses dt=1, because the embedding is trained on dt>1 transitions in the replay buffer. This creates a distinct failure mode: eval crashes first (policy destabilizes), entropy increases (actor loses certainty), and nve rises as a consequence of eval collapse rather than as a cause. The architectural vulnerability is the dt_embedding itself, not just the imagination process.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Pathway A vs Pathway B signature comparison\"\n        columns: [\"feature\", \"Pathway_A_008d_s1\", \"Pathway_B_008e_v3_s2\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"value_change\", \"+130% (60→138)\", \"+13% (339→384)\"]\n          - [\"eval_change\", \"-43% (146→83)\", \"-81% (715→137)\"]\n          - [\"nve_driver\", \"value numerator explosion\", \"eval denominator collapse\"]\n          - [\"actor_entropy\", \"↓ (-3.47→-4.88)\", \"↑ (-5.21→-3.83)\"]\n          - [\"mechanism\", \"critic overestimate → actor exploit\", \"policy destabilize → value lag\"]\n          - [\"onset_step\", \"25K-35K\", \"95K-105K\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Side-by-side eval+value trajectories: s1 smooth ascent to 870; s2 rises to 715 then crashes. Entropy subplot showing divergent entropy signatures.\"\n    analysis: \"Dual-pathway finding is the key novelty upgrade. Pathway A (classic CIVO) requires dt>1 in imagination and is eliminated by dt=1 restriction. Pathway B operates through the trained dt_embedding's influence on state representations in the world model, independent of imagination strategy. The 70K onset delay (Pathway B at 95K vs Pathway A at 25K) suggests slower accumulation mechanism — likely representation drift in RSSM states conditioned on trained dt_emb.\"\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt_emb, V-recovery) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_013\"\n    description: \"Replicate value growth suppression across seeds\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 740\n      seed1_nve_range: [0.55, 0.90]\n      seed1_collapse: false\n      seed1_zigzag_recovery: true\n      seed2_eval_105K: 690\n      seed2_nve_range: [0.50, 0.85]\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed2_zigzag_recovery: true\n      both_seeds_nve_below_1x: true\n      mean_eval_105K: 715\n      vs_baseline_105K: \"-22%\"\n    result_rationale: \"Untrained dt_embedding provides random, uninformative conditioning — the critic cannot learn to exploit precise dt>1 predictions because the embedding never adapts. Both seeds should show the characteristic value suppression pattern (nve consistently below 1.0x) with zigzag recovery behavior. Performance is lower than 008e_v3 (which achieves 95% baseline) because subsample OFF means no dt>1 data efficiency gain, but the agent still imagines at dt=2, accumulating prediction errors. The 008a configuration represents the protective floor: vulnerable to neither Pathway A nor B since the dt_embedding is never trained. Seed variance in final eval (~690-740) reflects the stochastic recovery timing of the zigzag pattern.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Walker Walk 008a multi-seed NVE suppression\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"seed0_nve\", \"seed0_eval\", \"seed1_nve\", \"seed1_eval\", \"seed2_nve\", \"seed2_eval\"]\n        rows:\n          - [55000, 0.93, 488, 0.88, 460, 0.82, 420]\n          - [65000, 0.66, 608, 0.72, 530, 0.70, 490]\n          - [75000, 0.75, 470, 0.68, 580, 0.65, 520]\n          - [85000, 0.68, 584, 0.62, 610, 0.58, 570]\n          - [95000, 0.79, 572, 0.75, 650, 0.72, 620]\n          - [105000, 0.59, 778, 0.55, 740, 0.50, 690]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Three-seed NVE band plot: all seeds confined within 0.5-0.93x envelope, never approaching 1.5x threshold. Zigzag eval trajectories overlaid showing independent recovery timing.\"\n    analysis: \"Consistent value growth suppression across 3 seeds strengthens C3 from 'single observation' to 'reproducible structural effect'. The nve<1.0x pattern is robust — the untrained dt_embedding structurally blocks the CIVO feedback loop regardless of initialization.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run baseline (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_020\"\n    description: \"Establish reference performance in second DMC environment\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_final_eval: 832\n      seed2_final_eval: 810\n      seed3_final_eval: 845\n      mean_final_eval: 829\n      std_final_eval: 18\n      convergence_step: 350000\n      ve_ratio_stable: 0.68\n      ve_ratio_stable_range: \"0.65-0.72\"\n      peak_eval_mean: 860\n    result_rationale: \"Cheetah Run is a standard DreamerV3 benchmark task. Running experiment (010a) already shows 732.9@265K with continued climbing, consistent with convergence around 800-850 by 400-500K steps. The best tier requires cheetah_baseline_final_eval in [750, 900]. Cheetah Run converges slightly below Walker Walk (~950) because the reward landscape is more continuous but the task has higher-dimensional coordination requirements. The v/e ratio should stabilize in a similar range (~0.65-0.72) as Walker Walk baselines, confirming consistent critic calibration across environments. Three seeds provide IQM + CI per Agarwal et al. 2021.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cheetah Run baseline learning curves (3 seeds)\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"seed1_eval\", \"seed2_eval\", \"seed3_eval\", \"mean\", \"baseline_ve\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 280, 260, 295, 278, 0.09]\n          - [55000, 450, 420, 470, 447, 0.35]\n          - [105000, 650, 630, 670, 650, 0.58]\n          - [155000, 750, 720, 760, 743, 0.64]\n          - [205000, 800, 780, 820, 800, 0.67]\n          - [255000, 820, 795, 835, 817, 0.68]\n          - [305000, 830, 805, 840, 825, 0.69]\n          - [355000, 835, 810, 845, 830, 0.69]\n          - [405000, 832, 808, 848, 829, 0.68]\n          - [505000, 832, 810, 845, 829, 0.68]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Three-seed learning curve with shaded IQM CI band. Horizontal dashed line at mean=829. Convergence around 350K steps.\"\n    analysis: \"Cheetah baseline establishes the denominator for all cross-environment comparisons. v/e stable at ~0.68 matches Walker Walk's ~0.69-0.72, indicating consistent critic calibration properties of DreamerV3's RSSM across environments. This is the reference for computing NVE in Cheetah experiments.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_021\"\n    description: \"Cross-environment CIVO replication — trained dt_embedding in Cheetah\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_peak: 2.8\n      seed1_nve_peak_step: 55000\n      seed1_eval_peak: 420\n      seed1_eval_final_105K: 185\n      seed1_collapse: true\n      seed1_pathway: \"A_CIVO\"\n      seed2_nve_peak: 1.95\n      seed2_nve_peak_step: 45000\n      seed2_eval_final_105K: 380\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed2_self_correction: true\n      seed3_nve_peak: 3.2\n      seed3_nve_peak_step: 65000\n      seed3_eval_final_105K: 95\n      seed3_collapse: true\n      seed3_pathway: \"A_CIVO\"\n      nve_above_1.5x_count: \"3/3\"\n      collapse_rate: \"2/3\"\n      mean_eval_degradation_vs_baseline: \"55%\"\n      cheetah_trained_dtEmb_nve_peak_max: 3.2\n      cross_env_nve_threshold_hit: true\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires cheetah_trained_dtEmb_nve_peak in [1.6, 3.5] and eval_degradation in [35, 70]%. All 3 seeds exceeding nve>1.5x (satisfying ≥2/3 hypothesis) demonstrates the CIVO mechanism generalizes from Walker to Cheetah despite different dynamics (contact-rich locomotion with different body morphology). The collapse rate 2/3 is within [0.33, 0.67] range. Cheetah's continuous reward structure (speed-based) may slightly amplify the CIVO effect vs Walker's sparse-ish reward, explaining the marginally higher nve peaks. The RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is architecture-intrinsic and environment-agnostic — the trained dt_embedding exploits imprecise state predictions regardless of what those states represent.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cheetah Run trained dt_emb NVE trajectories\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"s1_nve\", \"s1_eval\", \"s2_nve\", \"s2_eval\", \"s3_nve\", \"s3_eval\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 1.72, 240, 1.55, 230, 1.48, 255]\n          - [35000, 2.10, 320, 1.80, 310, 1.85, 340]\n          - [45000, 2.45, 400, 1.95, 380, 2.20, 420]\n          - [55000, 2.80, 350, 1.70, 370, 2.65, 380]\n          - [65000, 2.20, 280, 1.30, 365, 3.20, 250]\n          - [75000, 1.90, 220, 0.95, 370, 2.80, 160]\n          - [85000, 2.10, 200, 0.72, 375, 2.40, 120]\n          - [95000, 1.85, 190, 0.60, 380, 2.10, 100]\n          - [105000, 1.70, 185, 0.55, 380, 1.90, 95]\n      - title: \"Cross-environment CIVO comparison\"\n        columns: [\"metric\", \"Walker_Walk\", \"Cheetah_Run\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"seeds exceeding nve>1.5x\", \"2/2\", \"3/3\"]\n          - [\"collapse rate\", \"1/2 (50%)\", \"2/3 (67%)\"]\n          - [\"mean nve peak\", \"3.09\", \"2.65\"]\n          - [\"mean onset step (nve>1.5x)\", \"30K\", \"28K\"]\n          - [\"eval degradation at 105K\", \"71%\", \"55%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Three-seed NVE plot with 1.5x threshold. All three cross threshold. Two remain elevated (collapse), one self-corrects. Dual-panel with Walker Walk for cross-env comparison.\"\n    analysis: \"CIVO replicates cleanly: 3/3 seeds exceed nve>1.5x confirming the trained dt_embedding reliably triggers pathological overestimation in Cheetah Run. Collapse rate 2/3 is slightly higher than Walker's 1/2, possibly because Cheetah's continuous velocity reward provides more gradient signal for the critic to exploit. The mechanism is environment-agnostic — it depends on RSSM architecture, not task dynamics.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_022\"\n    description: \"Cross-environment causal control — removing dt_embedding in Cheetah\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_peak: 1.10\n      seed1_eval_105K: 420\n      seed2_nve_peak: 0.95\n      seed2_eval_105K: 380\n      seed3_nve_peak: 1.18\n      seed3_eval_105K: 350\n      nve_above_1.5x_count: \"0/3\"\n      collapse_count: \"0/3\"\n      mean_eval_105K: 383\n      vs_baseline_degradation: \"54%\"\n      reward_MAE_vs_baseline: \"60x\"\n    result_rationale: \"Without dt_embedding, the CIVO mechanism is structurally blocked — the critic has no channel to condition on dt and therefore cannot develop systematic overestimation for dt>1 trajectories. All 3 seeds staying below nve=1.5x extends the 4/4 Walker separation to 7/7 across environments (trained 5/5 exceed, untrained/absent 0/7 exceed). Performance degradation (~54% vs baseline) is expected because removing dt_embedding impairs reward prediction quality (same mechanism as Walker 008f: reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 transitions), but critically there is NO collapse. The degradation is stable and bounded.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cheetah Run no-dt_emb NVE confirmation\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"s1_nve\", \"s2_nve\", \"s3_nve\", \"mean_eval\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 1.10, 0.95, 1.05, 190]\n          - [45000, 0.80, 0.75, 0.82, 280]\n          - [65000, 0.60, 0.55, 0.65, 340]\n          - [85000, 0.50, 0.48, 0.55, 370]\n          - [105000, 0.45, 0.42, 0.50, 383]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"NVE band plot: all three seeds declining below 1.0x, converging toward 0.4-0.5x by 105K. 1.5x threshold line never approached.\"\n    analysis: \"Cross-environment causal control confirmed. The 1.5x threshold achieves perfect separation: 5/5 trained dt_emb seeds exceed it, 0/5 absent dt_emb seeds approach it. This 10/10 classification accuracy across Walker+Cheetah exceeds the ≥90% hypothesis requirement.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb + no subsample (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_023\"\n    description: \"Cross-environment value suppression with uninformative dt conditioning\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_range: [0.55, 0.88]\n      seed1_eval_105K: 620\n      seed2_nve_range: [0.50, 0.92]\n      seed2_eval_105K: 580\n      seed3_nve_range: [0.52, 0.85]\n      seed3_eval_105K: 640\n      mean_eval_105K: 613\n      vs_baseline: \"-26%\"\n      nve_always_below_1x: true\n      zigzag_recovery_pattern: true\n    result_rationale: \"Untrained dt_embedding in Cheetah Run should replicate Walker 008a's value suppression pattern. The uninformative random embedding prevents the critic from learning dt-dependent value estimates, blocking both Pathway A and B. Performance (~75% baseline) is better than dt_embed_dim=0 (~46% baseline) because the untrained embedding still provides a consistent conditioning signal that aids representation learning, even though it conveys no temporal information. The zigzag recovery pattern reflects the agent periodically finding good policies through exploration but lacking the value calibration to maintain them stably.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb value suppression\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"mean_nve\", \"mean_eval\", \"baseline_eval\"]\n        rows:\n          - [55000, 0.85, 380, 447]\n          - [65000, 0.70, 450, 520]\n          - [75000, 0.72, 480, 600]\n          - [85000, 0.65, 540, 660]\n          - [95000, 0.60, 580, 720]\n          - [105000, 0.55, 613, 650]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Three-seed band plot: NVE envelope 0.50-0.92x, consistently below 1.0x. Eval shows gradual ascent with zigzag variance.\"\n    analysis: \"Cross-environment replication of C3 (value growth suppression). The untrained dt_embedding is a structural blocker to CIVO independent of environment dynamics. Combined with Walker 008a (0.59-0.93x range), we have 4 seeds across 2 environments all showing NVE<1.0x throughout.\"\n\n  - name: \"RSSM prediction quality: dt=1 vs dt=2 transitions\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_030\"\n    description: \"Quantify RSSM fidelity gap at dt=2 vs dt=1 in Cheetah Run models\"\n    expected_results:\n      cheetah_baseline_dt1_obs_MSE: 11.2\n      cheetah_baseline_dt2_obs_MSE: 28.8\n      cheetah_baseline_obs_gap: 2.57\n      cheetah_baseline_dt1_reward_MAE: 0.015\n      cheetah_baseline_dt2_reward_MAE: 0.028\n      cheetah_baseline_reward_gap: 1.87\n      cheetah_trained_dtEmb_dt1_obs_MSE: 35.7\n      cheetah_trained_dtEmb_dt2_obs_MSE: 48.9\n      cheetah_trained_dtEmb_obs_gap: 1.37\n      cheetah_trained_dtEmb_dt1_reward_MAE: 0.065\n      cheetah_trained_dtEmb_dt2_reward_MAE: 0.195\n      cheetah_trained_dtEmb_reward_gap: 3.00\n      cheetah_no_dtEmb_dt1_obs_MSE: 7.5\n      cheetah_no_dtEmb_dt2_obs_MSE: 24.1\n      cheetah_no_dtEmb_obs_gap: 3.21\n      cheetah_no_dtEmb_dt1_reward_MAE: 0.82\n      cheetah_no_dtEmb_dt2_reward_MAE: 0.83\n      cheetah_no_dtEmb_reward_gap: 1.01\n    result_rationale: \"The RSSM fidelity gap is architecture-intrinsic: the RSSM's single-step transition model accumulates prediction error when asked to predict dt=2 transitions. In Cheetah Run, the pattern mirrors Walker Walk exactly — trained dt_emb model shows low obs gap ratio (misleading since absolute errors are 3x baseline) but high reward gap (3.0x), enabling the critic to develop systematically biased value estimates for dt>1 trajectories. No-dt_emb model shows equal reward at both dt levels (gap 1.01x) because it cannot discriminate temporal scales. Cheetah's slightly higher obs gap (2.57x vs Walker 2.04x) reflects the more complex state dynamics (velocity-dependent reward, limb coordination).\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"RSSM Fidelity Table (Cross-environment)\"\n        columns: [\"env\", \"model\", \"dt1_obs_MSE\", \"dt2_obs_MSE\", \"obs_gap\", \"dt1_reward_MAE\", \"dt2_reward_MAE\", \"reward_gap\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Walker\", \"baseline\", 13.49, 27.50, \"2.04x\", 0.0102, 0.0159, \"1.56x\"]\n          - [\"Walker\", \"trained_dtEmb\", 41.31, 53.67, \"1.30x\", 0.0568, 0.1579, \"2.78x\"]\n          - [\"Walker\", \"no_dtEmb\", 8.01, 22.04, \"2.75x\", 0.7469, 0.7526, \"1.01x\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah\", \"baseline\", 11.2, 28.8, \"2.57x\", 0.015, 0.028, \"1.87x\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah\", \"trained_dtEmb\", 35.7, 48.9, \"1.37x\", 0.065, 0.195, \"3.00x\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah\", \"no_dtEmb\", 7.5, 24.1, \"3.21x\", 0.82, 0.83, \"1.01x\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cross-environment RSSM fidelity comparison: grouped bar chart showing absolute errors + gap ratios for both environments. Same structural pattern across environments.\"\n    analysis: \"Cross-environment RSSM fidelity data confirms the mechanistic foundation is architecture-specific, not task-specific. The reward gap pattern (trained_dtEmb >2.7x, no_dtEmb ≈1.0x) is the direct causal link: trained dt_embedding enables the reward head to produce systematically biased predictions at dt>1, feeding the CIVO loop.\"\n\n  - name: \"Actor entropy analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_031\"\n    description: \"Characterize dual-pathway signatures via entropy divergence\"\n    expected_results:\n      pathway_A_entropy_direction: \"monotonically decreasing\"\n      pathway_A_entropy_range: [-3.5, -5.0]\n      pathway_A_interpretation: \"actor over-commits to exploiting overestimated critic\"\n      pathway_B_entropy_direction: \"increasing after stability\"\n      pathway_B_entropy_range: [-5.2, -3.8]\n      pathway_B_interpretation: \"actor loses certainty as policy destabilizes\"\n      healthy_entropy_range: [-4.5, -5.5]\n      healthy_entropy_stable: true\n      separation_metric_entropy_derivative: \">0 for Pathway B, <0 for Pathway A\"\n    result_rationale: \"Entropy signatures are the cleanest diagnostic for distinguishing Pathway A from Pathway B. In Pathway A (008d s1), the critic overestimates value for specific actions → actor concentrates on those actions → entropy decreases monotonically → over-committed actor crashes when reality doesn't match. In Pathway B (008e_v3 s2), something destabilizes the policy externally → actor loses its learned behavior → entropy increases as it reverts toward uniform policy → eval crashes. This entropy direction is the single strongest discriminator between the two pathways and generalizes to Cheetah where we expect the same signature separation.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Actor entropy trajectories by pathway\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"healthy_008e_v3_s1\", \"Pathway_A_008d_s1\", \"Pathway_B_008e_v3_s2\", \"baseline\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 0.17, -1.86, 0.17, -0.5]\n          - [45000, -4.92, -4.58, -4.92, -4.2]\n          - [65000, -5.14, -4.88, -5.14, -5.0]\n          - [85000, -5.21, \"collapsed\", -5.21, -5.1]\n          - [95000, -5.15, \"—\", -4.53, -5.1]\n          - [105000, -5.10, \"—\", -3.83, -5.1]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Entropy time-series: healthy configs stabilize at -5.0 to -5.2; Pathway A decreases below -4.5 before collapse; Pathway B shows reversal from -5.2 to -3.8 during collapse. Vertical dashed lines mark collapse onset for each pathway.\"\n    analysis: \"Entropy direction is a binary classifier: d(entropy)/dt < 0 at collapse onset → Pathway A (CIVO); d(entropy)/dt > 0 → Pathway B (policy instability). This enables real-time identification of which failure mode is active, with implications for targeted mitigation.\"\n\n  - name: \"Value prediction distribution vs realized returns\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_032\"\n    description: \"Distributional analysis showing where value estimates diverge from reality\"\n    expected_results:\n      healthy_v_vs_mc_correlation: 0.92\n      pathway_A_v_vs_mc_correlation: 0.45\n      pathway_A_overestimation_skew: \"+35% positive tail\"\n      pathway_B_v_vs_mc_correlation: 0.78\n      pathway_B_calibration_note: \"V accurate but policy diverges from predicted distribution\"\n      trained_dtEmb_dt2_overestimate_fraction: 0.82\n      untrained_dtEmb_dt2_overestimate_fraction: 0.48\n    result_rationale: \"In healthy configs, V(s) correlates well with MC returns (r~0.92). Pathway A shows the critic systematically overestimates, especially for states visited under dt>1 imagination — the trained dt_embedding produces optimistically biased value predictions, visible as a rightward skew in the V(s)-vs-MC scatter plot. Pathway B maintains reasonable value calibration (r~0.78) but the policy stops visiting the states the critic was calibrated on — value isn't wrong, the policy is. This distributional view directly demonstrates that Pathway A is critic-driven and Pathway B is actor-driven.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Value calibration summary\"\n        columns: [\"config\", \"V_MC_correlation\", \"mean_overestimation_%\", \"dt2_states_overestimate_frac\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"baseline (healthy)\", 0.92, \"+3%\", 0.52]\n          - [\"008d s1 @45K (pre-collapse)\", 0.45, \"+42%\", 0.82]\n          - [\"008d s1 @55K (collapsed)\", 0.22, \"+180%\", 0.95]\n          - [\"008e_v3 s2 @85K (pre-collapse)\", 0.78, \"+8%\", 0.55]\n          - [\"008e_v3 s2 @105K (collapsed)\", 0.60, \"+15%\", 0.58]\n          - [\"008a (untrained)\", 0.88, \"-5%\", 0.48]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Scatter plots: V(s) vs MC return for each config. Pathway A shows heavy positive bias above diagonal; Pathway B shows reasonable calibration but wider scatter.\"\n    analysis: \"Distributional analysis confirms the dual-pathway taxonomy: Pathway A = value overestimation (V>>MC, r drops to 0.22); Pathway B = value remains calibrated (V≈MC, r=0.60) but policy stops executing the actions the critic expects. This is the strongest evidence that 'conditioning-induced value overestimation' is specifically Pathway A's mechanism, not Pathway B's.\"\n\n  - name: \"dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_033\"\n    description: \"Track how dt=1 and dt=2 embedding vectors diverge during training\"\n    expected_results:\n      initial_cosine_sim: 0.15\n      trained_25K_cosine_sim: 0.08\n      trained_45K_cosine_sim: -0.12\n      trained_55K_cosine_sim_008d_collapsed: -0.35\n      trained_105K_cosine_sim_009b_selfcorrect: -0.08\n      untrained_cosine_sim: 0.15\n      frozen_cosine_sim: 0.15\n      correlation_with_nve: -0.78\n    result_rationale: \"As training progresses, the dt_embedding vectors for dt=1 and dt=2 should diverge (cosine similarity decreases) because the embedding is optimized to distinguish temporal scales for the observation decoders (93% of gradient). Greater divergence indicates the embedding has learned stronger dt-specific representations, which enables more precise (but incorrect at dt>1) conditioning. The negative correlation with NVE (r=-0.78) means larger embedding divergence predicts higher overestimation — the embedding's precision is exactly what enables the CIVO mechanism. In self-correcting seeds, embedding divergence stabilizes or partially reverses as the model readjusts. Frozen embeddings maintain their random initial similarity (~0.15) throughout.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"dt_embedding cosine similarity evolution\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"008d_s1_collapsed\", \"009b_s2_selfcorrect\", \"008a_untrained\", \"015b_frozen\"]\n        rows:\n          - [5000, 0.15, 0.15, 0.15, 0.15]\n          - [25000, 0.08, 0.10, 0.15, 0.15]\n          - [35000, -0.05, 0.02, 0.15, 0.15]\n          - [45000, -0.20, -0.08, 0.15, 0.15]\n          - [55000, -0.35, -0.12, 0.15, 0.15]\n          - [65000, \"collapsed\", -0.08, 0.15, 0.15]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cosine similarity time-series: collapsed seed rapidly diverges (negative similarity); self-correcting seed partially rebounds; untrained/frozen flat at initial value.\"\n    analysis: \"The dt_embedding's learned separation is the mechanistic enabler of CIVO. Greater embedding divergence = more precise dt conditioning = more exploitable for the critic. This provides a potential early-warning metric: monitoring cosine similarity could detect imminent CIVO before nve>1.5x threshold is crossed.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup baseline (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_050\"\n    description: \"Baseline in non-locomotion environment to broaden scope\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_final_eval: 820\n      seed2_final_eval: 795\n      seed3_final_eval: 840\n      mean_final_eval: 818\n      std_final_eval: 23\n      convergence_step: 300000\n      ve_ratio_stable: 0.66\n      ve_ratio_stable_range: \"0.63-0.70\"\n    result_rationale: \"Cartpole Swingup is a non-locomotion task with fundamentally different dynamics — a single joint, pendulum physics, sparse reward structure (reward only high when pole is balanced upright at full extension). DreamerV3 converges to ~800-860 on this task. The v/e ratio stabilizes in a similar range (0.63-0.70) to Walker and Cheetah, confirming that critic calibration properties are architecture-determined rather than task-determined. This environment broadens the claim from 'locomotion tasks' to 'DMC tasks in general', addressing reviewer concern R1.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cartpole Swingup baseline trajectories\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"seed1\", \"seed2\", \"seed3\", \"mean\", \"ve_ratio\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 180, 165, 195, 180, 0.10]\n          - [55000, 380, 350, 400, 377, 0.32]\n          - [105000, 620, 590, 640, 617, 0.55]\n          - [155000, 740, 710, 760, 737, 0.62]\n          - [205000, 790, 770, 810, 790, 0.65]\n          - [255000, 810, 785, 830, 808, 0.66]\n          - [305000, 820, 795, 840, 818, 0.66]\n          - [405000, 820, 795, 838, 818, 0.67]\n          - [505000, 820, 795, 840, 818, 0.66]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Three-seed learning curve with CI band. Convergence earlier than Walker/Cheetah (~300K) due to simpler dynamics.\"\n    analysis: \"Third environment baseline established. Faster convergence than locomotion tasks reflects the lower-dimensional control problem. Stable v/e ratio in the same 0.63-0.70 band confirms RSSM critic calibration is architecture-intrinsic.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_051\"\n    description: \"Cross-environment CIVO in non-locomotion task\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_peak: 2.4\n      seed1_eval_peak: 380\n      seed1_eval_final_105K: 250\n      seed1_collapse: true\n      seed1_pathway: \"A_CIVO\"\n      seed2_nve_peak: 1.75\n      seed2_eval_final_105K: 420\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed2_self_correction: true\n      seed3_nve_peak: 3.8\n      seed3_eval_peak: 350\n      seed3_eval_final_105K: 65\n      seed3_collapse: true\n      seed3_pathway: \"A_CIVO\"\n      nve_above_1.5x_count: \"3/3\"\n      collapse_rate: \"2/3\"\n      cartpole_trained_dtEmb_nve_peak_max: 3.8\n      mean_eval_degradation_vs_baseline: \"52%\"\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires cartpole_trained_dtEmb_nve_peak in [1.5, 4.0]. Cartpole Swingup has a sparser reward structure (binary-like: pole up or not), which may amplify CIVO severity because small policy errors cause large return drops, making the nve ratio spike more dramatically. 3/3 seeds exceeding nve>1.5x (satisfying ≥2/3 hypothesis) demonstrates that the vulnerability is truly architecture-specific and independent of task dynamics — whether locomotion (Walker, Cheetah) or balance (Cartpole). The highest nve peak (3.8x) reflects Cartpole's sensitivity to policy errors: a slightly wrong policy in Cartpole produces near-zero returns, inflating the nve denominator dramatically.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb NVE\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"s1_nve\", \"s1_eval\", \"s2_nve\", \"s2_eval\", \"s3_nve\", \"s3_eval\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 1.60, 160, 1.52, 155, 1.70, 170]\n          - [35000, 2.00, 280, 1.75, 290, 2.30, 260]\n          - [45000, 2.40, 380, 1.65, 350, 2.90, 350]\n          - [55000, 2.20, 320, 1.40, 380, 3.50, 280]\n          - [65000, 1.90, 280, 1.10, 400, 3.80, 150]\n          - [75000, 1.70, 260, 0.85, 410, 3.20, 100]\n          - [85000, 1.60, 255, 0.70, 415, 2.80, 80]\n          - [95000, 1.55, 252, 0.60, 420, 2.50, 70]\n          - [105000, 1.50, 250, 0.55, 420, 2.20, 65]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Three-environment CIVO comparison panel: Walker, Cheetah, Cartpole side by side showing same nve>1.5x pattern regardless of task.\"\n    analysis: \"Cross-environment generalization confirmed for third environment. 3/3 seeds exceed nve>1.5x threshold. The peak nve=3.8x in Cartpole (vs 3.2x Cheetah, 4.12x Walker) shows consistent severity range. Combined cross-env classification: 8/8 trained dt_emb seeds exceed 1.5x, 0/5 absent dt_emb seeds exceed — 13/13 correct if combining with plan_052 results.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_052\"\n    description: \"Cross-environment causal control in Cartpole\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_peak: 1.05\n      seed1_eval_105K: 380\n      seed2_nve_peak: 1.15\n      seed2_eval_105K: 350\n      seed3_nve_peak: 0.98\n      seed3_eval_105K: 400\n      nve_above_1.5x_count: \"0/3\"\n      collapse_count: \"0/3\"\n      mean_eval_105K: 377\n      vs_baseline_degradation: \"54%\"\n    result_rationale: \"Without dt_embedding, the causal control pattern holds in Cartpole as it does in Walker and Cheetah. The critic cannot develop dt-dependent overestimation without the conditioning channel. All 3 seeds remain below nve=1.5x, extending the cross-environment classification to 13/13 (8 trained exceed, 5+3=8 absent/untrained stay below — wait, let me recalculate: Walker trained 2/2 + Cheetah trained 3/3 + Cartpole trained 3/3 = 8/8 exceed; Walker absent 2/2 + Cheetah absent 3/3 + Cartpole absent 3/3 = 8/8 stay below = 16/16 total). Performance degradation from reward prediction impairment is consistent across environments (~50-55% baseline).\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cartpole no-dt_emb NVE\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"s1_nve\", \"s2_nve\", \"s3_nve\", \"mean_eval\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 1.05, 1.10, 0.98, 145]\n          - [45000, 0.78, 0.82, 0.75, 240]\n          - [65000, 0.60, 0.65, 0.58, 310]\n          - [85000, 0.50, 0.55, 0.48, 360]\n          - [105000, 0.45, 0.50, 0.42, 377]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Combined cross-environment NVE classification figure: all trained seeds above 1.5x line, all absent seeds below. Perfect separation.\"\n    analysis: \"16/16 perfect classification across 3 environments confirms the nve 1.5x threshold is a robust, environment-agnostic diagnostic for the presence of trained dt_embedding. Cross-environment accuracy = 100%, exceeding the ≥90% hypothesis requirement.\"\n\n  - name: \"NVE threshold sensitivity analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_034\"\n    description: \"Compute classification accuracy at multiple thresholds, report AUROC\"\n    expected_results:\n      threshold_1.3x_accuracy: 0.875\n      threshold_1.3x_FP: 1\n      threshold_1.3x_FN: 0\n      threshold_1.5x_accuracy: 1.0\n      threshold_1.5x_FP: 0\n      threshold_1.5x_FN: 0\n      threshold_1.7x_accuracy: 0.938\n      threshold_1.7x_FP: 0\n      threshold_1.7x_FN: 1\n      threshold_2.0x_accuracy: 0.875\n      threshold_2.0x_FP: 0\n      threshold_2.0x_FN: 2\n      AUROC: 0.98\n      optimal_threshold: 1.5\n      optimal_youden_J: 1.0\n      n_total_datapoints: 16\n      n_positive: 8\n      n_negative: 8\n    result_rationale: \"The 1.5x threshold is optimal because it sits in the natural gap between trained (minimum nve peak ~1.52x from Cartpole seed 2) and absent (maximum nve peak ~1.26x from Walker 009c). At 1.3x, one absent seed (Walker 009c peak 1.26x if we round up or another borderline case) becomes a false positive. At 1.7x, one trained seed (Cartpole seed 2 peak 1.75x with transient crossing) becomes a false negative if using sustained rather than peak criteria. 2.0x misses two self-correcting seeds that peaked between 1.5-2.0x. The AUROC of 0.98 reflects near-perfect separation with minimal overlap region. Cross-environment accuracy at 1.5x = 16/16 = 100%, exceeding the ≥11/12 hypothesis requirement.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"NVE threshold sensitivity (cross-environment)\"\n        columns: [\"threshold\", \"TP\", \"FP\", \"TN\", \"FN\", \"accuracy\", \"sensitivity\", \"specificity\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"1.3x\", 8, 1, 7, 0, \"93.8%\", \"100%\", \"87.5%\"]\n          - [\"1.5x\", 8, 0, 8, 0, \"100%\", \"100%\", \"100%\"]\n          - [\"1.7x\", 7, 0, 8, 1, \"93.8%\", \"87.5%\", \"100%\"]\n          - [\"2.0x\", 6, 0, 8, 2, \"87.5%\", \"75%\", \"100%\"]\n      - title: \"Peak NVE values for all seeds\"\n        columns: [\"experiment\", \"env\", \"trained_dtEmb\", \"peak_nve_35K+\", \"classification_1.5x\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"008d s1\", \"Walker\", \"yes\", 2.09, \"TP\"]\n          - [\"009b s2\", \"Walker\", \"yes\", 1.62, \"TP\"]\n          - [\"008f s1\", \"Walker\", \"no\", 1.02, \"TN\"]\n          - [\"009c s2\", \"Walker\", \"no\", 1.26, \"TN\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah s1\", \"Cheetah\", \"yes\", 2.80, \"TP\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah s2\", \"Cheetah\", \"yes\", 1.95, \"TP\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah s3\", \"Cheetah\", \"yes\", 3.20, \"TP\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah ctrl s1\", \"Cheetah\", \"no\", 1.10, \"TN\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah ctrl s2\", \"Cheetah\", \"no\", 0.95, \"TN\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah ctrl s3\", \"Cheetah\", \"no\", 1.18, \"TN\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole s1\", \"Cartpole\", \"yes\", 2.40, \"TP\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole s2\", \"Cartpole\", \"yes\", 1.75, \"TP\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole s3\", \"Cartpole\", \"yes\", 3.80, \"TP\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole ctrl s1\", \"Cartpole\", \"no\", 1.05, \"TN\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole ctrl s2\", \"Cartpole\", \"no\", 1.15, \"TN\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole ctrl s3\", \"Cartpole\", \"no\", 0.98, \"TN\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"ROC curve with AUROC=0.98. Inset: histogram of peak NVE values showing bimodal distribution with clear separation at 1.5x.\"\n    analysis: \"The 1.5x threshold achieves perfect Youden's J = 1.0 (sensitivity + specificity - 1), indicating no overlap between classes at this threshold. This is the paper's strongest quantitative contribution: a simple, environment-agnostic diagnostic that perfectly separates CIVO-vulnerable configurations from safe ones.\"\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk FROZEN dt_emb + subsample ON + dt=2 imag + H=8\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_015\"\n    description: \"Diagnose whether dt_emb TRAINING is necessary for CIVO\"\n    expected_results:\n      eval_105K: 204\n      nve_range: [0.39, 0.47]\n      collapse: false\n      mechanism: \"gradient_starvation\"\n      actor_loss_approaching_zero: true\n      gate_dt_mean: 2.0\n      interpretation: \"CIVO requires trained dt_emb — frozen random dt_emb does not trigger overestimation but causes gradient starvation (actor_loss→0)\"\n    result_rationale: \"A frozen random dt_embedding provides consistent but uninformative conditioning. Unlike the untrained dt_emb in 008a (which is trained but unexposed to dt>1), the frozen embedding cannot adapt at all. This creates a distinct failure mode: gradient starvation. The dt_embedding feeds into the world model's posterior but never adapts, creating a persistent noise floor that prevents clean gradient propagation to the actor. NVE stays well below 1.0x (0.39-0.47x) because the critic under-estimates rather than over-estimates — it cannot learn accurate value predictions with the noisy frozen conditioning. This confirms trained dt_embedding is necessary for CIVO (not just any dt_embedding), and distinguishes 'trained' from 'present but frozen'.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Frozen vs trained vs absent dt_emb comparison\"\n        columns: [\"config\", \"dt_emb_state\", \"nve_peak\", \"collapse\", \"mechanism\", \"eval_105K\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"008d\", \"trained\", \"4.12x\", \"yes (1/2)\", \"CIVO\", 131]\n          - [\"015b\", \"frozen_random\", \"0.47x\", \"no\", \"gradient_starvation\", 204]\n          - [\"008f\", \"absent (dim=0)\", \"1.02x\", \"no\", \"reward_degradation\", 503]\n          - [\"008a\", \"trained_unexposed\", \"0.93x\", \"no\", \"value_suppression\", 778]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Four-way comparison: eval trajectories + NVE for trained/frozen/absent/unexposed dt_emb configurations.\"\n    analysis: \"Frozen dt_emb isolates the TRAINING of the embedding as necessary for CIVO. The 2×2 matrix is now complete: {trained, exposed to dt>1} = CIVO; {trained, unexposed} = suppression; {frozen} = starvation; {absent} = degradation. Only the intersection of trained AND exposed triggers the overestimation feedback loop.\"\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 additional seeds (seed 3-4)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_016\"\n    description: \"Narrow dual-pathway collapse rate estimate\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed3_eval_105K: 830\n      seed3_nve_105K: 0.75\n      seed3_pathway: \"healthy\"\n      seed3_collapse: false\n      seed4_eval_105K: 180\n      seed4_nve_105K: 2.95\n      seed4_pathway: \"B_policy_instability\"\n      seed4_collapse: true\n      seed4_entropy_direction: \"increasing\"\n      overall_collapse_rate: \"2/4 (50%)\"\n      pathway_B_count: \"2/4\"\n      pathway_A_count: \"0/4\"\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires the dual-pathway finding to be characterizable across multiple observations. With 4 independent seeds of 008e_v3 (dt=1 imagination + trained dt_emb), a 2/4 collapse rate confirms ~50% Pathway B vulnerability. Critically, ALL collapses are Pathway B (entropy increasing, eval-driven) and NONE are Pathway A (CIVO is eliminated by dt=1 imagination). This cleanly demonstrates that dt=1 imagination is a perfect Pathway A blocker but provides no protection against Pathway B. Seed 4 collapses late (95K+) with the characteristic entropy rise signature, matching 008e_v3_s2. Seed 3 remains healthy like s1. The 50% rate suggests a bifurcation point in early training that determines long-term stability.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"008e_v3 all seeds summary\"\n        columns: [\"seed\", \"eval_105K\", \"nve_105K\", \"collapse\", \"pathway\", \"entropy_95-105K\"]\n        rows:\n          - [1, 870, 0.80, \"no\", \"healthy\", \"-5.15 → -5.10\"]\n          - [2, 137, 3.78, \"yes\", \"B (policy instability)\", \"-5.21 → -3.83\"]\n          - [3, 830, 0.75, \"no\", \"healthy\", \"-5.10 → -5.05\"]\n          - [4, 180, 2.95, \"yes\", \"B (policy instability)\", \"-5.18 → -4.05\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Four-seed overlay: healthy seeds plateau at ~830-870; collapsed seeds crash from ~700 to <200. Entropy subplot showing the bifurcation clearly.\"\n    analysis: \"2/4 collapse rate confirms Pathway B is a genuine ~50% risk with trained dt_embedding, independent of imagination dt. The consistent entropy-rising signature across both collapsed seeds (s2: -5.21→-3.83, s4: -5.18→-4.05) establishes Pathway B as a reproducible, characterizable failure mode distinct from CIVO.\"\n\n  - name: \"Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_060\"\n    description: \"Pilot: truncate imagination when RSSM uncertainty exceeds threshold\"\n    expected_results:\n      eval_105K: 520\n      nve_peak: 1.35\n      nve_above_1.5x: false\n      collapse: false\n      mean_effective_horizon: 5.2\n      truncation_rate_dt2_steps: 0.45\n      vs_baseline: \"-43%\"\n      vs_008d_improvement: \"collapse prevented\"\n    result_rationale: \"Uncertainty-gated truncation addresses CIVO's root cause: the RSSM's fidelity gap at dt>1 allows the critic to be misled. By monitoring RSSM prediction uncertainty (e.g., ensemble disagreement or posterior variance) and truncating imagination when uncertainty exceeds a threshold, we prevent the critic from training on high-uncertainty dt>1 predictions. This should eliminate CIVO (nve stays below 1.5x) but at the cost of reduced effective imagination horizon (mean ~5.2 vs nominal 8), leading to slower learning. Performance at 105K is moderate (520, ~56% baseline) but collapse-free. This is a proof-of-concept, not a final solution — the truncation threshold is hand-tuned and conservative.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Uncertainty truncation pilot vs controls\"\n        columns: [\"config\", \"eval_105K\", \"nve_peak\", \"collapse\", \"effective_H\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"baseline (dt=1, H=15)\", 916, \"0.66x\", \"no\", 15]\n          - [\"008d (trained, dt=2, H=8)\", 131, \"4.12x\", \"yes\", 8]\n          - [\"uncertainty_gated (dt=2, H=8)\", 520, \"1.35x\", \"no\", 5.2]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Learning curve: uncertainty-gated between baseline and 008d. Effective horizon histogram showing truncation distribution.\"\n    analysis: \"Proof-of-concept successful: uncertainty gating prevents CIVO by truncating before the RSSM's prediction quality degrades enough to mislead the critic. The cost is reduced effective horizon. Future work: adaptive thresholds, learned truncation, connection to THICK/Hieros hierarchical approaches.\"\n\n  - name: \"Hopper Hop validation (3 seeds × 3 configs)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_040\"\n    description: \"Fourth environment for strongest generalization claim\"\n    expected_results:\n      baseline_mean_final: 280\n      trained_dtEmb_nve_above_1.5x: \"2/3\"\n      trained_dtEmb_collapse_rate: \"1/3\"\n      no_dtEmb_nve_above_1.5x: \"0/3\"\n      trained_dtEmb_mean_nve_peak: 2.1\n      classification_accuracy_1.5x: \"6/6\"\n    result_rationale: \"Hopper Hop is the hardest standard DMC task with lower baseline performance (~250-300). Even in this challenging setting, the CIVO mechanism operates identically: trained dt_embedding triggers nve>1.5x in ≥2/3 seeds while absent dt_embedding maintains nve<1.5x. The lower absolute performance makes nve ratios slightly noisier (smaller denominators), but the 1.5x threshold still separates perfectly (6/6 in this environment). Combined cross-environment accuracy: 22/22 (100%), far exceeding the 90% requirement. Collapse rate is slightly lower (1/3) because Hopper Hop's lower baseline returns mean the positive feedback loop has less absolute value to exploit.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Hopper Hop CIVO generalization\"\n        columns: [\"config\", \"s1_nve_peak\", \"s2_nve_peak\", \"s3_nve_peak\", \"collapse_rate\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"trained_dtEmb\", 2.5, 1.8, 2.1, \"1/3\"]\n          - [\"no_dtEmb\", 1.10, 0.95, 1.20, \"0/3\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Four-environment summary figure: Walker, Cheetah, Cartpole, Hopper showing consistent CIVO pattern across all tasks.\"\n    analysis: \"Fourth environment further strengthens the architecture-specificity claim. Total cross-environment NVE classification: 22/22 correct (11 trained seeds all exceed 1.5x, 11 control seeds all stay below). The mechanism is conclusively environment-agnostic.\"\n\n  - name: \"Full-SGS (observation decoder gradient detach) Walker Walk\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_018_implicit\"\n    description: \"exp_018 — detach observation decoder gradients from dt_emb (93% of gradient pathway)\"\n    expected_results:\n      eval_105K: 350\n      nve_peak: 1.25\n      nve_above_1.5x: false\n      collapse: false\n      dt_emb_grad_norm_remaining: 0.119\n      dt_emb_grad_source: \"KL dynamics only (7%)\"\n      vs_008d: \"CIVO prevented\"\n      vs_baseline: \"-62%\"\n      interpretation: \"Removing observation decoder gradients from dt_emb eliminates CIVO, confirming the gradient pathway analysis\"\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires Full-SGS to show meaningful intervention effect. Since dt_emb receives 93% of its gradient from observation decoders and 7% from KL dynamics, detaching the observation pathway leaves only the weak KL signal. This should dramatically reduce the embedding's ability to learn precise dt distinctions, preventing the CIVO mechanism. The nve stays below 1.5x (predicted 1.25x peak) because the residual 7% KL gradient is insufficient to create the precise dt conditioning that enables value exploitation. Performance is lower than baseline (350 vs 916) because the intervention also impairs the world model's dt-awareness for state prediction, similar to 008f's reward degradation but through a different mechanism. The key finding: this experimentally confirms that the observation decoder→dt_emb gradient pathway is the causal enabler of CIVO.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Gradient intervention comparison\"\n        columns: [\"config\", \"dt_emb_grad_fraction_removed\", \"nve_peak\", \"collapse\", \"eval_105K\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"008d (no intervention)\", \"0%\", \"4.12x\", \"yes\", 131]\n          - [\"016e (reward detach = no-op)\", \"0% (reward grad = 0)\", \"10.42x\", \"yes\", 32]\n          - [\"018 (obs decoder detach)\", \"93%\", \"1.25x\", \"no\", 350]\n          - [\"008f (dt_emb absent)\", \"100%\", \"1.02x\", \"no\", 503]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Gradient intervention dose-response: x-axis = fraction of dt_emb gradient removed, y-axis = peak NVE. Clear threshold between 0% (CIVO) and 93% (no CIVO).\"\n    analysis: \"Full-SGS experimentally validates the gradient decomposition analysis from D061. The 93% observation decoder gradient is both necessary and (nearly) sufficient for CIVO. Removing it reduces NVE from pathological (>4x) to healthy (1.25x). This is the mechanistic smoking gun: the dt_embedding learns precise temporal distinctions through observation reconstruction, and this precision is what enables the critic exploitation feedback loop.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":40578,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":40578},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c5c7d6db-7a89-4828-b637-4d0ede508a4e","uuid":"2607329e-b293-4746-9b0b-e4ca14611aed"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":368846,"duration_api_ms":370328,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: best\nnarrative_guidance: \"Architecture-specific vulnerability generalizes across DMC tasks — trained dt-embedding is a reliable CIVO trigger in imagination-based actor-critic regardless of environment dynamics. TAWM's MPC success vs our imagination failure creates a clean architectural distinction. Dual-pathway finding elevates contribution from 'one failure mode' to 'systematic fragility taxonomy'. Paper framing unchanged: strong diagnostic analysis with actionable architectural guidance.\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008d (trained dt_emb, collapse) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_011\"\n    description: \"Replicate CIVO collapse with 2 additional seeds on Walker Walk\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_peak: 4.12\n      seed1_nve_peak_step: 55000\n      seed1_eval_105K: 131\n      seed1_collapse: true\n      seed1_collapse_onset: 25000\n      seed2_nve_peak: 2.05\n      seed2_nve_peak_step: 25000\n      seed2_eval_105K: 268\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed2_self_correction: true\n      nve_above_1.5x_count: \"2/2\"\n      collapse_rate: \"1/2\"\n      cross_seed_nve_separation: 1.0\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires walker_additional_seeds_nve_separation=[1.0,1.0] — perfect 2/2 seeds exceeding nve>1.5x at 35K+. Collapse remains stochastic (1/2) because the feedback loop lock-in depends on early training stochasticity (initialization, replay buffer sampling order). Seed 1 sustains nve>1.5x across consecutive checkpoints (positive feedback locks), seed 2 transiently exceeds then self-corrects as actor gradient signal from real returns overwhelms overestimated critic. This 100% diagnostic marker + stochastic collapse pattern is the strongest quantitative separation for C2.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Walker Walk 008d multi-seed NVE trajectory\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"seed1_nve\", \"seed1_eval\", \"seed2_nve\", \"seed2_eval\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 1.78, 167, 2.05, 141]\n          - [35000, 1.71, 146, 1.24, 164]\n          - [45000, 2.09, 141, 1.62, 141]\n          - [55000, 4.12, 83, 1.41, 172]\n          - [65000, 1.23, 250, 0.94, 209]\n          - [75000, 1.48, 178, 0.66, 197]\n          - [85000, 1.15, 202, 0.51, 236]\n          - [95000, 1.73, 133, 0.43, 279]\n          - [105000, 1.57, 131, 0.38, 268]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"NVE time-series: seed 1 sustained above 1.5x dashed line (35K-55K) then partial recovery but re-collapse; seed 2 briefly touches 1.5x then monotonically declines. Shaded threshold region.\"\n    analysis: \"Perfect 2/2 diagnostic separation — both trained dt_emb seeds exceed nve>1.5x at 35K+. Collapse probability 1/2 is within expected range [0.33, 0.67]. The bifurcation between sustained vs transient overestimation at 35-55K is the critical window determining outcome.\"\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008f (dt_embed_dim=0, stable) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_012\"\n    description: \"Replicate causal control stability — no dt_embedding → no CIVO\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_peak: 1.02\n      seed1_nve_peak_step: 25000\n      seed1_eval_105K: 503\n      seed1_collapse: false\n      seed2_nve_peak: 1.26\n      seed2_nve_peak_step: 25000\n      seed2_eval_105K: 148\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      nve_above_1.5x_count: \"0/2\"\n      causal_direction_confirmed: true\n    result_rationale: \"Without dt_embedding conditioning, the value function cannot exploit precise dt predictions in imagination. The RSSM still produces dt>1 transitions in training data (subsample ON), but without the conditioning channel, the critic receives no signal to systematically overestimate dt>1 trajectories. Both seeds remain below nve=1.5x throughout, confirming dt_embedding is the necessary amplifier. Seed 2 shows declining eval after 75K due to the inherent difficulty of H=8 learning without dt-awareness for reward prediction (reward_MAE=0.747, 75x baseline), but this is performance limitation, not CIVO collapse.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Walker Walk 008f causal control NVE (both seeds)\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"seed1_nve\", \"seed1_eval\", \"seed2_nve\", \"seed2_eval\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 1.02, 197, 1.26, 169]\n          - [35000, 0.73, 207, 0.79, 179]\n          - [45000, 0.83, 194, 0.72, 211]\n          - [55000, 0.97, 173, 0.66, 239]\n          - [65000, 0.37, 375, 0.53, 244]\n          - [75000, 0.42, 324, 0.33, 359]\n          - [85000, 0.65, 246, 0.60, 227]\n          - [95000, 0.54, 319, 0.90, 191]\n          - [105000, 0.30, 503, 1.14, 148]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"NVE trajectory: both seeds confined below 1.5x threshold (dashed line never crossed). Contrast with 008d seeds where both cross 1.5x before 45K.\"\n    analysis: \"4/4 perfect separation maintained: trained dt_emb 2/2 exceed 1.5x, untrained/absent 0/2 exceed. 008f's performance variance across seeds (503 vs 148) reflects the cost of removing dt_embedding — reward prediction degradation limits learning stability — but the CIVO mechanism is completely blocked.\"\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 (dt=1 imagination) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_014\"\n    description: \"Multi-seed validation revealing dual-pathway vulnerability\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 870\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.80\n      seed1_pathway: \"healthy\"\n      seed2_eval_105K: 137\n      seed2_nve_105K: 3.78\n      seed2_pathway: \"B_policy_instability\"\n      seed2_entropy_direction: \"increasing (-5.21→-3.83)\"\n      collapse_rate: \"1/2\"\n      pathway_a_present: false\n      pathway_b_present: true\n      dual_pathway_confirmed: true\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires dual-pathway finding characterizable with distinct signatures. Seed 1 is healthy because dt=1 imagination eliminates Pathway A (CIVO requires dt>1 imagination for value overestimation feedback loop). Seed 2 collapses via Pathway B — the trained dt_embedding corrupts the critic's state representation even when imagination uses dt=1, because the embedding is trained on dt>1 transitions in the replay buffer. This creates a distinct failure mode: eval crashes first (policy destabilizes), entropy increases (actor loses certainty), and nve rises as a consequence of eval collapse rather than as a cause. The architectural vulnerability is the dt_embedding itself, not just the imagination process.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Pathway A vs Pathway B signature comparison\"\n        columns: [\"feature\", \"Pathway_A_008d_s1\", \"Pathway_B_008e_v3_s2\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"value_change\", \"+130% (60→138)\", \"+13% (339→384)\"]\n          - [\"eval_change\", \"-43% (146→83)\", \"-81% (715→137)\"]\n          - [\"nve_driver\", \"value numerator explosion\", \"eval denominator collapse\"]\n          - [\"actor_entropy\", \"↓ (-3.47→-4.88)\", \"↑ (-5.21→-3.83)\"]\n          - [\"mechanism\", \"critic overestimate → actor exploit\", \"policy destabilize → value lag\"]\n          - [\"onset_step\", \"25K-35K\", \"95K-105K\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Side-by-side eval+value trajectories: s1 smooth ascent to 870; s2 rises to 715 then crashes. Entropy subplot showing divergent entropy signatures.\"\n    analysis: \"Dual-pathway finding is the key novelty upgrade. Pathway A (classic CIVO) requires dt>1 in imagination and is eliminated by dt=1 restriction. Pathway B operates through the trained dt_embedding's influence on state representations in the world model, independent of imagination strategy. The 70K onset delay (Pathway B at 95K vs Pathway A at 25K) suggests slower accumulation mechanism — likely representation drift in RSSM states conditioned on trained dt_emb.\"\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt_emb, V-recovery) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_013\"\n    description: \"Replicate value growth suppression across seeds\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 740\n      seed1_nve_range: [0.55, 0.90]\n      seed1_collapse: false\n      seed1_zigzag_recovery: true\n      seed2_eval_105K: 690\n      seed2_nve_range: [0.50, 0.85]\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed2_zigzag_recovery: true\n      both_seeds_nve_below_1x: true\n      mean_eval_105K: 715\n      vs_baseline_105K: \"-22%\"\n    result_rationale: \"Untrained dt_embedding provides random, uninformative conditioning — the critic cannot learn to exploit precise dt>1 predictions because the embedding never adapts. Both seeds should show the characteristic value suppression pattern (nve consistently below 1.0x) with zigzag recovery behavior. Performance is lower than 008e_v3 (which achieves 95% baseline) because subsample OFF means no dt>1 data efficiency gain, but the agent still imagines at dt=2, accumulating prediction errors. The 008a configuration represents the protective floor: vulnerable to neither Pathway A nor B since the dt_embedding is never trained. Seed variance in final eval (~690-740) reflects the stochastic recovery timing of the zigzag pattern.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Walker Walk 008a multi-seed NVE suppression\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"seed0_nve\", \"seed0_eval\", \"seed1_nve\", \"seed1_eval\", \"seed2_nve\", \"seed2_eval\"]\n        rows:\n          - [55000, 0.93, 488, 0.88, 460, 0.82, 420]\n          - [65000, 0.66, 608, 0.72, 530, 0.70, 490]\n          - [75000, 0.75, 470, 0.68, 580, 0.65, 520]\n          - [85000, 0.68, 584, 0.62, 610, 0.58, 570]\n          - [95000, 0.79, 572, 0.75, 650, 0.72, 620]\n          - [105000, 0.59, 778, 0.55, 740, 0.50, 690]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Three-seed NVE band plot: all seeds confined within 0.5-0.93x envelope, never approaching 1.5x threshold. Zigzag eval trajectories overlaid showing independent recovery timing.\"\n    analysis: \"Consistent value growth suppression across 3 seeds strengthens C3 from 'single observation' to 'reproducible structural effect'. The nve<1.0x pattern is robust — the untrained dt_embedding structurally blocks the CIVO feedback loop regardless of initialization.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run baseline (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_020\"\n    description: \"Establish reference performance in second DMC environment\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_final_eval: 832\n      seed2_final_eval: 810\n      seed3_final_eval: 845\n      mean_final_eval: 829\n      std_final_eval: 18\n      convergence_step: 350000\n      ve_ratio_stable: 0.68\n      ve_ratio_stable_range: \"0.65-0.72\"\n      peak_eval_mean: 860\n    result_rationale: \"Cheetah Run is a standard DreamerV3 benchmark task. Running experiment (010a) already shows 732.9@265K with continued climbing, consistent with convergence around 800-850 by 400-500K steps. The best tier requires cheetah_baseline_final_eval in [750, 900]. Cheetah Run converges slightly below Walker Walk (~950) because the reward landscape is more continuous but the task has higher-dimensional coordination requirements. The v/e ratio should stabilize in a similar range (~0.65-0.72) as Walker Walk baselines, confirming consistent critic calibration across environments. Three seeds provide IQM + CI per Agarwal et al. 2021.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cheetah Run baseline learning curves (3 seeds)\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"seed1_eval\", \"seed2_eval\", \"seed3_eval\", \"mean\", \"baseline_ve\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 280, 260, 295, 278, 0.09]\n          - [55000, 450, 420, 470, 447, 0.35]\n          - [105000, 650, 630, 670, 650, 0.58]\n          - [155000, 750, 720, 760, 743, 0.64]\n          - [205000, 800, 780, 820, 800, 0.67]\n          - [255000, 820, 795, 835, 817, 0.68]\n          - [305000, 830, 805, 840, 825, 0.69]\n          - [355000, 835, 810, 845, 830, 0.69]\n          - [405000, 832, 808, 848, 829, 0.68]\n          - [505000, 832, 810, 845, 829, 0.68]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Three-seed learning curve with shaded IQM CI band. Horizontal dashed line at mean=829. Convergence around 350K steps.\"\n    analysis: \"Cheetah baseline establishes the denominator for all cross-environment comparisons. v/e stable at ~0.68 matches Walker Walk's ~0.69-0.72, indicating consistent critic calibration properties of DreamerV3's RSSM across environments. This is the reference for computing NVE in Cheetah experiments.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_021\"\n    description: \"Cross-environment CIVO replication — trained dt_embedding in Cheetah\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_peak: 2.8\n      seed1_nve_peak_step: 55000\n      seed1_eval_peak: 420\n      seed1_eval_final_105K: 185\n      seed1_collapse: true\n      seed1_pathway: \"A_CIVO\"\n      seed2_nve_peak: 1.95\n      seed2_nve_peak_step: 45000\n      seed2_eval_final_105K: 380\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed2_self_correction: true\n      seed3_nve_peak: 3.2\n      seed3_nve_peak_step: 65000\n      seed3_eval_final_105K: 95\n      seed3_collapse: true\n      seed3_pathway: \"A_CIVO\"\n      nve_above_1.5x_count: \"3/3\"\n      collapse_rate: \"2/3\"\n      mean_eval_degradation_vs_baseline: \"55%\"\n      cheetah_trained_dtEmb_nve_peak_max: 3.2\n      cross_env_nve_threshold_hit: true\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires cheetah_trained_dtEmb_nve_peak in [1.6, 3.5] and eval_degradation in [35, 70]%. All 3 seeds exceeding nve>1.5x (satisfying ≥2/3 hypothesis) demonstrates the CIVO mechanism generalizes from Walker to Cheetah despite different dynamics (contact-rich locomotion with different body morphology). The collapse rate 2/3 is within [0.33, 0.67] range. Cheetah's continuous reward structure (speed-based) may slightly amplify the CIVO effect vs Walker's sparse-ish reward, explaining the marginally higher nve peaks. The RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is architecture-intrinsic and environment-agnostic — the trained dt_embedding exploits imprecise state predictions regardless of what those states represent.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cheetah Run trained dt_emb NVE trajectories\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"s1_nve\", \"s1_eval\", \"s2_nve\", \"s2_eval\", \"s3_nve\", \"s3_eval\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 1.72, 240, 1.55, 230, 1.48, 255]\n          - [35000, 2.10, 320, 1.80, 310, 1.85, 340]\n          - [45000, 2.45, 400, 1.95, 380, 2.20, 420]\n          - [55000, 2.80, 350, 1.70, 370, 2.65, 380]\n          - [65000, 2.20, 280, 1.30, 365, 3.20, 250]\n          - [75000, 1.90, 220, 0.95, 370, 2.80, 160]\n          - [85000, 2.10, 200, 0.72, 375, 2.40, 120]\n          - [95000, 1.85, 190, 0.60, 380, 2.10, 100]\n          - [105000, 1.70, 185, 0.55, 380, 1.90, 95]\n      - title: \"Cross-environment CIVO comparison\"\n        columns: [\"metric\", \"Walker_Walk\", \"Cheetah_Run\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"seeds exceeding nve>1.5x\", \"2/2\", \"3/3\"]\n          - [\"collapse rate\", \"1/2 (50%)\", \"2/3 (67%)\"]\n          - [\"mean nve peak\", \"3.09\", \"2.65\"]\n          - [\"mean onset step (nve>1.5x)\", \"30K\", \"28K\"]\n          - [\"eval degradation at 105K\", \"71%\", \"55%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Three-seed NVE plot with 1.5x threshold. All three cross threshold. Two remain elevated (collapse), one self-corrects. Dual-panel with Walker Walk for cross-env comparison.\"\n    analysis: \"CIVO replicates cleanly: 3/3 seeds exceed nve>1.5x confirming the trained dt_embedding reliably triggers pathological overestimation in Cheetah Run. Collapse rate 2/3 is slightly higher than Walker's 1/2, possibly because Cheetah's continuous velocity reward provides more gradient signal for the critic to exploit. The mechanism is environment-agnostic — it depends on RSSM architecture, not task dynamics.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_022\"\n    description: \"Cross-environment causal control — removing dt_embedding in Cheetah\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_peak: 1.10\n      seed1_eval_105K: 420\n      seed2_nve_peak: 0.95\n      seed2_eval_105K: 380\n      seed3_nve_peak: 1.18\n      seed3_eval_105K: 350\n      nve_above_1.5x_count: \"0/3\"\n      collapse_count: \"0/3\"\n      mean_eval_105K: 383\n      vs_baseline_degradation: \"54%\"\n      reward_MAE_vs_baseline: \"60x\"\n    result_rationale: \"Without dt_embedding, the CIVO mechanism is structurally blocked — the critic has no channel to condition on dt and therefore cannot develop systematic overestimation for dt>1 trajectories. All 3 seeds staying below nve=1.5x extends the 4/4 Walker separation to 7/7 across environments (trained 5/5 exceed, untrained/absent 0/7 exceed). Performance degradation (~54% vs baseline) is expected because removing dt_embedding impairs reward prediction quality (same mechanism as Walker 008f: reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 transitions), but critically there is NO collapse. The degradation is stable and bounded.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cheetah Run no-dt_emb NVE confirmation\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"s1_nve\", \"s2_nve\", \"s3_nve\", \"mean_eval\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 1.10, 0.95, 1.05, 190]\n          - [45000, 0.80, 0.75, 0.82, 280]\n          - [65000, 0.60, 0.55, 0.65, 340]\n          - [85000, 0.50, 0.48, 0.55, 370]\n          - [105000, 0.45, 0.42, 0.50, 383]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"NVE band plot: all three seeds declining below 1.0x, converging toward 0.4-0.5x by 105K. 1.5x threshold line never approached.\"\n    analysis: \"Cross-environment causal control confirmed. The 1.5x threshold achieves perfect separation: 5/5 trained dt_emb seeds exceed it, 0/5 absent dt_emb seeds approach it. This 10/10 classification accuracy across Walker+Cheetah exceeds the ≥90% hypothesis requirement.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb + no subsample (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_023\"\n    description: \"Cross-environment value suppression with uninformative dt conditioning\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_range: [0.55, 0.88]\n      seed1_eval_105K: 620\n      seed2_nve_range: [0.50, 0.92]\n      seed2_eval_105K: 580\n      seed3_nve_range: [0.52, 0.85]\n      seed3_eval_105K: 640\n      mean_eval_105K: 613\n      vs_baseline: \"-26%\"\n      nve_always_below_1x: true\n      zigzag_recovery_pattern: true\n    result_rationale: \"Untrained dt_embedding in Cheetah Run should replicate Walker 008a's value suppression pattern. The uninformative random embedding prevents the critic from learning dt-dependent value estimates, blocking both Pathway A and B. Performance (~75% baseline) is better than dt_embed_dim=0 (~46% baseline) because the untrained embedding still provides a consistent conditioning signal that aids representation learning, even though it conveys no temporal information. The zigzag recovery pattern reflects the agent periodically finding good policies through exploration but lacking the value calibration to maintain them stably.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb value suppression\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"mean_nve\", \"mean_eval\", \"baseline_eval\"]\n        rows:\n          - [55000, 0.85, 380, 447]\n          - [65000, 0.70, 450, 520]\n          - [75000, 0.72, 480, 600]\n          - [85000, 0.65, 540, 660]\n          - [95000, 0.60, 580, 720]\n          - [105000, 0.55, 613, 650]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Three-seed band plot: NVE envelope 0.50-0.92x, consistently below 1.0x. Eval shows gradual ascent with zigzag variance.\"\n    analysis: \"Cross-environment replication of C3 (value growth suppression). The untrained dt_embedding is a structural blocker to CIVO independent of environment dynamics. Combined with Walker 008a (0.59-0.93x range), we have 4 seeds across 2 environments all showing NVE<1.0x throughout.\"\n\n  - name: \"RSSM prediction quality: dt=1 vs dt=2 transitions\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_030\"\n    description: \"Quantify RSSM fidelity gap at dt=2 vs dt=1 in Cheetah Run models\"\n    expected_results:\n      cheetah_baseline_dt1_obs_MSE: 11.2\n      cheetah_baseline_dt2_obs_MSE: 28.8\n      cheetah_baseline_obs_gap: 2.57\n      cheetah_baseline_dt1_reward_MAE: 0.015\n      cheetah_baseline_dt2_reward_MAE: 0.028\n      cheetah_baseline_reward_gap: 1.87\n      cheetah_trained_dtEmb_dt1_obs_MSE: 35.7\n      cheetah_trained_dtEmb_dt2_obs_MSE: 48.9\n      cheetah_trained_dtEmb_obs_gap: 1.37\n      cheetah_trained_dtEmb_dt1_reward_MAE: 0.065\n      cheetah_trained_dtEmb_dt2_reward_MAE: 0.195\n      cheetah_trained_dtEmb_reward_gap: 3.00\n      cheetah_no_dtEmb_dt1_obs_MSE: 7.5\n      cheetah_no_dtEmb_dt2_obs_MSE: 24.1\n      cheetah_no_dtEmb_obs_gap: 3.21\n      cheetah_no_dtEmb_dt1_reward_MAE: 0.82\n      cheetah_no_dtEmb_dt2_reward_MAE: 0.83\n      cheetah_no_dtEmb_reward_gap: 1.01\n    result_rationale: \"The RSSM fidelity gap is architecture-intrinsic: the RSSM's single-step transition model accumulates prediction error when asked to predict dt=2 transitions. In Cheetah Run, the pattern mirrors Walker Walk exactly — trained dt_emb model shows low obs gap ratio (misleading since absolute errors are 3x baseline) but high reward gap (3.0x), enabling the critic to develop systematically biased value estimates for dt>1 trajectories. No-dt_emb model shows equal reward at both dt levels (gap 1.01x) because it cannot discriminate temporal scales. Cheetah's slightly higher obs gap (2.57x vs Walker 2.04x) reflects the more complex state dynamics (velocity-dependent reward, limb coordination).\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"RSSM Fidelity Table (Cross-environment)\"\n        columns: [\"env\", \"model\", \"dt1_obs_MSE\", \"dt2_obs_MSE\", \"obs_gap\", \"dt1_reward_MAE\", \"dt2_reward_MAE\", \"reward_gap\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Walker\", \"baseline\", 13.49, 27.50, \"2.04x\", 0.0102, 0.0159, \"1.56x\"]\n          - [\"Walker\", \"trained_dtEmb\", 41.31, 53.67, \"1.30x\", 0.0568, 0.1579, \"2.78x\"]\n          - [\"Walker\", \"no_dtEmb\", 8.01, 22.04, \"2.75x\", 0.7469, 0.7526, \"1.01x\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah\", \"baseline\", 11.2, 28.8, \"2.57x\", 0.015, 0.028, \"1.87x\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah\", \"trained_dtEmb\", 35.7, 48.9, \"1.37x\", 0.065, 0.195, \"3.00x\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah\", \"no_dtEmb\", 7.5, 24.1, \"3.21x\", 0.82, 0.83, \"1.01x\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cross-environment RSSM fidelity comparison: grouped bar chart showing absolute errors + gap ratios for both environments. Same structural pattern across environments.\"\n    analysis: \"Cross-environment RSSM fidelity data confirms the mechanistic foundation is architecture-specific, not task-specific. The reward gap pattern (trained_dtEmb >2.7x, no_dtEmb ≈1.0x) is the direct causal link: trained dt_embedding enables the reward head to produce systematically biased predictions at dt>1, feeding the CIVO loop.\"\n\n  - name: \"Actor entropy analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_031\"\n    description: \"Characterize dual-pathway signatures via entropy divergence\"\n    expected_results:\n      pathway_A_entropy_direction: \"monotonically decreasing\"\n      pathway_A_entropy_range: [-3.5, -5.0]\n      pathway_A_interpretation: \"actor over-commits to exploiting overestimated critic\"\n      pathway_B_entropy_direction: \"increasing after stability\"\n      pathway_B_entropy_range: [-5.2, -3.8]\n      pathway_B_interpretation: \"actor loses certainty as policy destabilizes\"\n      healthy_entropy_range: [-4.5, -5.5]\n      healthy_entropy_stable: true\n      separation_metric_entropy_derivative: \">0 for Pathway B, <0 for Pathway A\"\n    result_rationale: \"Entropy signatures are the cleanest diagnostic for distinguishing Pathway A from Pathway B. In Pathway A (008d s1), the critic overestimates value for specific actions → actor concentrates on those actions → entropy decreases monotonically → over-committed actor crashes when reality doesn't match. In Pathway B (008e_v3 s2), something destabilizes the policy externally → actor loses its learned behavior → entropy increases as it reverts toward uniform policy → eval crashes. This entropy direction is the single strongest discriminator between the two pathways and generalizes to Cheetah where we expect the same signature separation.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Actor entropy trajectories by pathway\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"healthy_008e_v3_s1\", \"Pathway_A_008d_s1\", \"Pathway_B_008e_v3_s2\", \"baseline\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 0.17, -1.86, 0.17, -0.5]\n          - [45000, -4.92, -4.58, -4.92, -4.2]\n          - [65000, -5.14, -4.88, -5.14, -5.0]\n          - [85000, -5.21, \"collapsed\", -5.21, -5.1]\n          - [95000, -5.15, \"—\", -4.53, -5.1]\n          - [105000, -5.10, \"—\", -3.83, -5.1]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Entropy time-series: healthy configs stabilize at -5.0 to -5.2; Pathway A decreases below -4.5 before collapse; Pathway B shows reversal from -5.2 to -3.8 during collapse. Vertical dashed lines mark collapse onset for each pathway.\"\n    analysis: \"Entropy direction is a binary classifier: d(entropy)/dt < 0 at collapse onset → Pathway A (CIVO); d(entropy)/dt > 0 → Pathway B (policy instability). This enables real-time identification of which failure mode is active, with implications for targeted mitigation.\"\n\n  - name: \"Value prediction distribution vs realized returns\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_032\"\n    description: \"Distributional analysis showing where value estimates diverge from reality\"\n    expected_results:\n      healthy_v_vs_mc_correlation: 0.92\n      pathway_A_v_vs_mc_correlation: 0.45\n      pathway_A_overestimation_skew: \"+35% positive tail\"\n      pathway_B_v_vs_mc_correlation: 0.78\n      pathway_B_calibration_note: \"V accurate but policy diverges from predicted distribution\"\n      trained_dtEmb_dt2_overestimate_fraction: 0.82\n      untrained_dtEmb_dt2_overestimate_fraction: 0.48\n    result_rationale: \"In healthy configs, V(s) correlates well with MC returns (r~0.92). Pathway A shows the critic systematically overestimates, especially for states visited under dt>1 imagination — the trained dt_embedding produces optimistically biased value predictions, visible as a rightward skew in the V(s)-vs-MC scatter plot. Pathway B maintains reasonable value calibration (r~0.78) but the policy stops visiting the states the critic was calibrated on — value isn't wrong, the policy is. This distributional view directly demonstrates that Pathway A is critic-driven and Pathway B is actor-driven.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Value calibration summary\"\n        columns: [\"config\", \"V_MC_correlation\", \"mean_overestimation_%\", \"dt2_states_overestimate_frac\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"baseline (healthy)\", 0.92, \"+3%\", 0.52]\n          - [\"008d s1 @45K (pre-collapse)\", 0.45, \"+42%\", 0.82]\n          - [\"008d s1 @55K (collapsed)\", 0.22, \"+180%\", 0.95]\n          - [\"008e_v3 s2 @85K (pre-collapse)\", 0.78, \"+8%\", 0.55]\n          - [\"008e_v3 s2 @105K (collapsed)\", 0.60, \"+15%\", 0.58]\n          - [\"008a (untrained)\", 0.88, \"-5%\", 0.48]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Scatter plots: V(s) vs MC return for each config. Pathway A shows heavy positive bias above diagonal; Pathway B shows reasonable calibration but wider scatter.\"\n    analysis: \"Distributional analysis confirms the dual-pathway taxonomy: Pathway A = value overestimation (V>>MC, r drops to 0.22); Pathway B = value remains calibrated (V≈MC, r=0.60) but policy stops executing the actions the critic expects. This is the strongest evidence that 'conditioning-induced value overestimation' is specifically Pathway A's mechanism, not Pathway B's.\"\n\n  - name: \"dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_033\"\n    description: \"Track how dt=1 and dt=2 embedding vectors diverge during training\"\n    expected_results:\n      initial_cosine_sim: 0.15\n      trained_25K_cosine_sim: 0.08\n      trained_45K_cosine_sim: -0.12\n      trained_55K_cosine_sim_008d_collapsed: -0.35\n      trained_105K_cosine_sim_009b_selfcorrect: -0.08\n      untrained_cosine_sim: 0.15\n      frozen_cosine_sim: 0.15\n      correlation_with_nve: -0.78\n    result_rationale: \"As training progresses, the dt_embedding vectors for dt=1 and dt=2 should diverge (cosine similarity decreases) because the embedding is optimized to distinguish temporal scales for the observation decoders (93% of gradient). Greater divergence indicates the embedding has learned stronger dt-specific representations, which enables more precise (but incorrect at dt>1) conditioning. The negative correlation with NVE (r=-0.78) means larger embedding divergence predicts higher overestimation — the embedding's precision is exactly what enables the CIVO mechanism. In self-correcting seeds, embedding divergence stabilizes or partially reverses as the model readjusts. Frozen embeddings maintain their random initial similarity (~0.15) throughout.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"dt_embedding cosine similarity evolution\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"008d_s1_collapsed\", \"009b_s2_selfcorrect\", \"008a_untrained\", \"015b_frozen\"]\n        rows:\n          - [5000, 0.15, 0.15, 0.15, 0.15]\n          - [25000, 0.08, 0.10, 0.15, 0.15]\n          - [35000, -0.05, 0.02, 0.15, 0.15]\n          - [45000, -0.20, -0.08, 0.15, 0.15]\n          - [55000, -0.35, -0.12, 0.15, 0.15]\n          - [65000, \"collapsed\", -0.08, 0.15, 0.15]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cosine similarity time-series: collapsed seed rapidly diverges (negative similarity); self-correcting seed partially rebounds; untrained/frozen flat at initial value.\"\n    analysis: \"The dt_embedding's learned separation is the mechanistic enabler of CIVO. Greater embedding divergence = more precise dt conditioning = more exploitable for the critic. This provides a potential early-warning metric: monitoring cosine similarity could detect imminent CIVO before nve>1.5x threshold is crossed.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup baseline (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_050\"\n    description: \"Baseline in non-locomotion environment to broaden scope\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_final_eval: 820\n      seed2_final_eval: 795\n      seed3_final_eval: 840\n      mean_final_eval: 818\n      std_final_eval: 23\n      convergence_step: 300000\n      ve_ratio_stable: 0.66\n      ve_ratio_stable_range: \"0.63-0.70\"\n    result_rationale: \"Cartpole Swingup is a non-locomotion task with fundamentally different dynamics — a single joint, pendulum physics, sparse reward structure (reward only high when pole is balanced upright at full extension). DreamerV3 converges to ~800-860 on this task. The v/e ratio stabilizes in a similar range (0.63-0.70) to Walker and Cheetah, confirming that critic calibration properties are architecture-determined rather than task-determined. This environment broadens the claim from 'locomotion tasks' to 'DMC tasks in general', addressing reviewer concern R1.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cartpole Swingup baseline trajectories\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"seed1\", \"seed2\", \"seed3\", \"mean\", \"ve_ratio\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 180, 165, 195, 180, 0.10]\n          - [55000, 380, 350, 400, 377, 0.32]\n          - [105000, 620, 590, 640, 617, 0.55]\n          - [155000, 740, 710, 760, 737, 0.62]\n          - [205000, 790, 770, 810, 790, 0.65]\n          - [255000, 810, 785, 830, 808, 0.66]\n          - [305000, 820, 795, 840, 818, 0.66]\n          - [405000, 820, 795, 838, 818, 0.67]\n          - [505000, 820, 795, 840, 818, 0.66]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Three-seed learning curve with CI band. Convergence earlier than Walker/Cheetah (~300K) due to simpler dynamics.\"\n    analysis: \"Third environment baseline established. Faster convergence than locomotion tasks reflects the lower-dimensional control problem. Stable v/e ratio in the same 0.63-0.70 band confirms RSSM critic calibration is architecture-intrinsic.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_051\"\n    description: \"Cross-environment CIVO in non-locomotion task\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_peak: 2.4\n      seed1_eval_peak: 380\n      seed1_eval_final_105K: 250\n      seed1_collapse: true\n      seed1_pathway: \"A_CIVO\"\n      seed2_nve_peak: 1.75\n      seed2_eval_final_105K: 420\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed2_self_correction: true\n      seed3_nve_peak: 3.8\n      seed3_eval_peak: 350\n      seed3_eval_final_105K: 65\n      seed3_collapse: true\n      seed3_pathway: \"A_CIVO\"\n      nve_above_1.5x_count: \"3/3\"\n      collapse_rate: \"2/3\"\n      cartpole_trained_dtEmb_nve_peak_max: 3.8\n      mean_eval_degradation_vs_baseline: \"52%\"\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires cartpole_trained_dtEmb_nve_peak in [1.5, 4.0]. Cartpole Swingup has a sparser reward structure (binary-like: pole up or not), which may amplify CIVO severity because small policy errors cause large return drops, making the nve ratio spike more dramatically. 3/3 seeds exceeding nve>1.5x (satisfying ≥2/3 hypothesis) demonstrates that the vulnerability is truly architecture-specific and independent of task dynamics — whether locomotion (Walker, Cheetah) or balance (Cartpole). The highest nve peak (3.8x) reflects Cartpole's sensitivity to policy errors: a slightly wrong policy in Cartpole produces near-zero returns, inflating the nve denominator dramatically.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb NVE\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"s1_nve\", \"s1_eval\", \"s2_nve\", \"s2_eval\", \"s3_nve\", \"s3_eval\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 1.60, 160, 1.52, 155, 1.70, 170]\n          - [35000, 2.00, 280, 1.75, 290, 2.30, 260]\n          - [45000, 2.40, 380, 1.65, 350, 2.90, 350]\n          - [55000, 2.20, 320, 1.40, 380, 3.50, 280]\n          - [65000, 1.90, 280, 1.10, 400, 3.80, 150]\n          - [75000, 1.70, 260, 0.85, 410, 3.20, 100]\n          - [85000, 1.60, 255, 0.70, 415, 2.80, 80]\n          - [95000, 1.55, 252, 0.60, 420, 2.50, 70]\n          - [105000, 1.50, 250, 0.55, 420, 2.20, 65]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Three-environment CIVO comparison panel: Walker, Cheetah, Cartpole side by side showing same nve>1.5x pattern regardless of task.\"\n    analysis: \"Cross-environment generalization confirmed for third environment. 3/3 seeds exceed nve>1.5x threshold. The peak nve=3.8x in Cartpole (vs 3.2x Cheetah, 4.12x Walker) shows consistent severity range. Combined cross-env classification: 8/8 trained dt_emb seeds exceed 1.5x, 0/5 absent dt_emb seeds exceed — 13/13 correct if combining with plan_052 results.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_052\"\n    description: \"Cross-environment causal control in Cartpole\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_peak: 1.05\n      seed1_eval_105K: 380\n      seed2_nve_peak: 1.15\n      seed2_eval_105K: 350\n      seed3_nve_peak: 0.98\n      seed3_eval_105K: 400\n      nve_above_1.5x_count: \"0/3\"\n      collapse_count: \"0/3\"\n      mean_eval_105K: 377\n      vs_baseline_degradation: \"54%\"\n    result_rationale: \"Without dt_embedding, the causal control pattern holds in Cartpole as it does in Walker and Cheetah. The critic cannot develop dt-dependent overestimation without the conditioning channel. All 3 seeds remain below nve=1.5x, extending the cross-environment classification to 13/13 (8 trained exceed, 5+3=8 absent/untrained stay below — wait, let me recalculate: Walker trained 2/2 + Cheetah trained 3/3 + Cartpole trained 3/3 = 8/8 exceed; Walker absent 2/2 + Cheetah absent 3/3 + Cartpole absent 3/3 = 8/8 stay below = 16/16 total). Performance degradation from reward prediction impairment is consistent across environments (~50-55% baseline).\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cartpole no-dt_emb NVE\"\n        columns: [\"step\", \"s1_nve\", \"s2_nve\", \"s3_nve\", \"mean_eval\"]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 1.05, 1.10, 0.98, 145]\n          - [45000, 0.78, 0.82, 0.75, 240]\n          - [65000, 0.60, 0.65, 0.58, 310]\n          - [85000, 0.50, 0.55, 0.48, 360]\n          - [105000, 0.45, 0.50, 0.42, 377]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Combined cross-environment NVE classification figure: all trained seeds above 1.5x line, all absent seeds below. Perfect separation.\"\n    analysis: \"16/16 perfect classification across 3 environments confirms the nve 1.5x threshold is a robust, environment-agnostic diagnostic for the presence of trained dt_embedding. Cross-environment accuracy = 100%, exceeding the ≥90% hypothesis requirement.\"\n\n  - name: \"NVE threshold sensitivity analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_034\"\n    description: \"Compute classification accuracy at multiple thresholds, report AUROC\"\n    expected_results:\n      threshold_1.3x_accuracy: 0.875\n      threshold_1.3x_FP: 1\n      threshold_1.3x_FN: 0\n      threshold_1.5x_accuracy: 1.0\n      threshold_1.5x_FP: 0\n      threshold_1.5x_FN: 0\n      threshold_1.7x_accuracy: 0.938\n      threshold_1.7x_FP: 0\n      threshold_1.7x_FN: 1\n      threshold_2.0x_accuracy: 0.875\n      threshold_2.0x_FP: 0\n      threshold_2.0x_FN: 2\n      AUROC: 0.98\n      optimal_threshold: 1.5\n      optimal_youden_J: 1.0\n      n_total_datapoints: 16\n      n_positive: 8\n      n_negative: 8\n    result_rationale: \"The 1.5x threshold is optimal because it sits in the natural gap between trained (minimum nve peak ~1.52x from Cartpole seed 2) and absent (maximum nve peak ~1.26x from Walker 009c). At 1.3x, one absent seed (Walker 009c peak 1.26x if we round up or another borderline case) becomes a false positive. At 1.7x, one trained seed (Cartpole seed 2 peak 1.75x with transient crossing) becomes a false negative if using sustained rather than peak criteria. 2.0x misses two self-correcting seeds that peaked between 1.5-2.0x. The AUROC of 0.98 reflects near-perfect separation with minimal overlap region. Cross-environment accuracy at 1.5x = 16/16 = 100%, exceeding the ≥11/12 hypothesis requirement.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"NVE threshold sensitivity (cross-environment)\"\n        columns: [\"threshold\", \"TP\", \"FP\", \"TN\", \"FN\", \"accuracy\", \"sensitivity\", \"specificity\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"1.3x\", 8, 1, 7, 0, \"93.8%\", \"100%\", \"87.5%\"]\n          - [\"1.5x\", 8, 0, 8, 0, \"100%\", \"100%\", \"100%\"]\n          - [\"1.7x\", 7, 0, 8, 1, \"93.8%\", \"87.5%\", \"100%\"]\n          - [\"2.0x\", 6, 0, 8, 2, \"87.5%\", \"75%\", \"100%\"]\n      - title: \"Peak NVE values for all seeds\"\n        columns: [\"experiment\", \"env\", \"trained_dtEmb\", \"peak_nve_35K+\", \"classification_1.5x\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"008d s1\", \"Walker\", \"yes\", 2.09, \"TP\"]\n          - [\"009b s2\", \"Walker\", \"yes\", 1.62, \"TP\"]\n          - [\"008f s1\", \"Walker\", \"no\", 1.02, \"TN\"]\n          - [\"009c s2\", \"Walker\", \"no\", 1.26, \"TN\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah s1\", \"Cheetah\", \"yes\", 2.80, \"TP\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah s2\", \"Cheetah\", \"yes\", 1.95, \"TP\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah s3\", \"Cheetah\", \"yes\", 3.20, \"TP\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah ctrl s1\", \"Cheetah\", \"no\", 1.10, \"TN\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah ctrl s2\", \"Cheetah\", \"no\", 0.95, \"TN\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah ctrl s3\", \"Cheetah\", \"no\", 1.18, \"TN\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole s1\", \"Cartpole\", \"yes\", 2.40, \"TP\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole s2\", \"Cartpole\", \"yes\", 1.75, \"TP\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole s3\", \"Cartpole\", \"yes\", 3.80, \"TP\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole ctrl s1\", \"Cartpole\", \"no\", 1.05, \"TN\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole ctrl s2\", \"Cartpole\", \"no\", 1.15, \"TN\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole ctrl s3\", \"Cartpole\", \"no\", 0.98, \"TN\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"ROC curve with AUROC=0.98. Inset: histogram of peak NVE values showing bimodal distribution with clear separation at 1.5x.\"\n    analysis: \"The 1.5x threshold achieves perfect Youden's J = 1.0 (sensitivity + specificity - 1), indicating no overlap between classes at this threshold. This is the paper's strongest quantitative contribution: a simple, environment-agnostic diagnostic that perfectly separates CIVO-vulnerable configurations from safe ones.\"\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk FROZEN dt_emb + subsample ON + dt=2 imag + H=8\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_015\"\n    description: \"Diagnose whether dt_emb TRAINING is necessary for CIVO\"\n    expected_results:\n      eval_105K: 204\n      nve_range: [0.39, 0.47]\n      collapse: false\n      mechanism: \"gradient_starvation\"\n      actor_loss_approaching_zero: true\n      gate_dt_mean: 2.0\n      interpretation: \"CIVO requires trained dt_emb — frozen random dt_emb does not trigger overestimation but causes gradient starvation (actor_loss→0)\"\n    result_rationale: \"A frozen random dt_embedding provides consistent but uninformative conditioning. Unlike the untrained dt_emb in 008a (which is trained but unexposed to dt>1), the frozen embedding cannot adapt at all. This creates a distinct failure mode: gradient starvation. The dt_embedding feeds into the world model's posterior but never adapts, creating a persistent noise floor that prevents clean gradient propagation to the actor. NVE stays well below 1.0x (0.39-0.47x) because the critic under-estimates rather than over-estimates — it cannot learn accurate value predictions with the noisy frozen conditioning. This confirms trained dt_embedding is necessary for CIVO (not just any dt_embedding), and distinguishes 'trained' from 'present but frozen'.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Frozen vs trained vs absent dt_emb comparison\"\n        columns: [\"config\", \"dt_emb_state\", \"nve_peak\", \"collapse\", \"mechanism\", \"eval_105K\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"008d\", \"trained\", \"4.12x\", \"yes (1/2)\", \"CIVO\", 131]\n          - [\"015b\", \"frozen_random\", \"0.47x\", \"no\", \"gradient_starvation\", 204]\n          - [\"008f\", \"absent (dim=0)\", \"1.02x\", \"no\", \"reward_degradation\", 503]\n          - [\"008a\", \"trained_unexposed\", \"0.93x\", \"no\", \"value_suppression\", 778]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Four-way comparison: eval trajectories + NVE for trained/frozen/absent/unexposed dt_emb configurations.\"\n    analysis: \"Frozen dt_emb isolates the TRAINING of the embedding as necessary for CIVO. The 2×2 matrix is now complete: {trained, exposed to dt>1} = CIVO; {trained, unexposed} = suppression; {frozen} = starvation; {absent} = degradation. Only the intersection of trained AND exposed triggers the overestimation feedback loop.\"\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 additional seeds (seed 3-4)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_016\"\n    description: \"Narrow dual-pathway collapse rate estimate\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed3_eval_105K: 830\n      seed3_nve_105K: 0.75\n      seed3_pathway: \"healthy\"\n      seed3_collapse: false\n      seed4_eval_105K: 180\n      seed4_nve_105K: 2.95\n      seed4_pathway: \"B_policy_instability\"\n      seed4_collapse: true\n      seed4_entropy_direction: \"increasing\"\n      overall_collapse_rate: \"2/4 (50%)\"\n      pathway_B_count: \"2/4\"\n      pathway_A_count: \"0/4\"\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires the dual-pathway finding to be characterizable across multiple observations. With 4 independent seeds of 008e_v3 (dt=1 imagination + trained dt_emb), a 2/4 collapse rate confirms ~50% Pathway B vulnerability. Critically, ALL collapses are Pathway B (entropy increasing, eval-driven) and NONE are Pathway A (CIVO is eliminated by dt=1 imagination). This cleanly demonstrates that dt=1 imagination is a perfect Pathway A blocker but provides no protection against Pathway B. Seed 4 collapses late (95K+) with the characteristic entropy rise signature, matching 008e_v3_s2. Seed 3 remains healthy like s1. The 50% rate suggests a bifurcation point in early training that determines long-term stability.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"008e_v3 all seeds summary\"\n        columns: [\"seed\", \"eval_105K\", \"nve_105K\", \"collapse\", \"pathway\", \"entropy_95-105K\"]\n        rows:\n          - [1, 870, 0.80, \"no\", \"healthy\", \"-5.15 → -5.10\"]\n          - [2, 137, 3.78, \"yes\", \"B (policy instability)\", \"-5.21 → -3.83\"]\n          - [3, 830, 0.75, \"no\", \"healthy\", \"-5.10 → -5.05\"]\n          - [4, 180, 2.95, \"yes\", \"B (policy instability)\", \"-5.18 → -4.05\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Four-seed overlay: healthy seeds plateau at ~830-870; collapsed seeds crash from ~700 to <200. Entropy subplot showing the bifurcation clearly.\"\n    analysis: \"2/4 collapse rate confirms Pathway B is a genuine ~50% risk with trained dt_embedding, independent of imagination dt. The consistent entropy-rising signature across both collapsed seeds (s2: -5.21→-3.83, s4: -5.18→-4.05) establishes Pathway B as a reproducible, characterizable failure mode distinct from CIVO.\"\n\n  - name: \"Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_060\"\n    description: \"Pilot: truncate imagination when RSSM uncertainty exceeds threshold\"\n    expected_results:\n      eval_105K: 520\n      nve_peak: 1.35\n      nve_above_1.5x: false\n      collapse: false\n      mean_effective_horizon: 5.2\n      truncation_rate_dt2_steps: 0.45\n      vs_baseline: \"-43%\"\n      vs_008d_improvement: \"collapse prevented\"\n    result_rationale: \"Uncertainty-gated truncation addresses CIVO's root cause: the RSSM's fidelity gap at dt>1 allows the critic to be misled. By monitoring RSSM prediction uncertainty (e.g., ensemble disagreement or posterior variance) and truncating imagination when uncertainty exceeds a threshold, we prevent the critic from training on high-uncertainty dt>1 predictions. This should eliminate CIVO (nve stays below 1.5x) but at the cost of reduced effective imagination horizon (mean ~5.2 vs nominal 8), leading to slower learning. Performance at 105K is moderate (520, ~56% baseline) but collapse-free. This is a proof-of-concept, not a final solution — the truncation threshold is hand-tuned and conservative.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Uncertainty truncation pilot vs controls\"\n        columns: [\"config\", \"eval_105K\", \"nve_peak\", \"collapse\", \"effective_H\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"baseline (dt=1, H=15)\", 916, \"0.66x\", \"no\", 15]\n          - [\"008d (trained, dt=2, H=8)\", 131, \"4.12x\", \"yes\", 8]\n          - [\"uncertainty_gated (dt=2, H=8)\", 520, \"1.35x\", \"no\", 5.2]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Learning curve: uncertainty-gated between baseline and 008d. Effective horizon histogram showing truncation distribution.\"\n    analysis: \"Proof-of-concept successful: uncertainty gating prevents CIVO by truncating before the RSSM's prediction quality degrades enough to mislead the critic. The cost is reduced effective horizon. Future work: adaptive thresholds, learned truncation, connection to THICK/Hieros hierarchical approaches.\"\n\n  - name: \"Hopper Hop validation (3 seeds × 3 configs)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_040\"\n    description: \"Fourth environment for strongest generalization claim\"\n    expected_results:\n      baseline_mean_final: 280\n      trained_dtEmb_nve_above_1.5x: \"2/3\"\n      trained_dtEmb_collapse_rate: \"1/3\"\n      no_dtEmb_nve_above_1.5x: \"0/3\"\n      trained_dtEmb_mean_nve_peak: 2.1\n      classification_accuracy_1.5x: \"6/6\"\n    result_rationale: \"Hopper Hop is the hardest standard DMC task with lower baseline performance (~250-300). Even in this challenging setting, the CIVO mechanism operates identically: trained dt_embedding triggers nve>1.5x in ≥2/3 seeds while absent dt_embedding maintains nve<1.5x. The lower absolute performance makes nve ratios slightly noisier (smaller denominators), but the 1.5x threshold still separates perfectly (6/6 in this environment). Combined cross-environment accuracy: 22/22 (100%), far exceeding the 90% requirement. Collapse rate is slightly lower (1/3) because Hopper Hop's lower baseline returns mean the positive feedback loop has less absolute value to exploit.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Hopper Hop CIVO generalization\"\n        columns: [\"config\", \"s1_nve_peak\", \"s2_nve_peak\", \"s3_nve_peak\", \"collapse_rate\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"trained_dtEmb\", 2.5, 1.8, 2.1, \"1/3\"]\n          - [\"no_dtEmb\", 1.10, 0.95, 1.20, \"0/3\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Four-environment summary figure: Walker, Cheetah, Cartpole, Hopper showing consistent CIVO pattern across all tasks.\"\n    analysis: \"Fourth environment further strengthens the architecture-specificity claim. Total cross-environment NVE classification: 22/22 correct (11 trained seeds all exceed 1.5x, 11 control seeds all stay below). The mechanism is conclusively environment-agnostic.\"\n\n  - name: \"Full-SGS (observation decoder gradient detach) Walker Walk\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_018_implicit\"\n    description: \"exp_018 — detach observation decoder gradients from dt_emb (93% of gradient pathway)\"\n    expected_results:\n      eval_105K: 350\n      nve_peak: 1.25\n      nve_above_1.5x: false\n      collapse: false\n      dt_emb_grad_norm_remaining: 0.119\n      dt_emb_grad_source: \"KL dynamics only (7%)\"\n      vs_008d: \"CIVO prevented\"\n      vs_baseline: \"-62%\"\n      interpretation: \"Removing observation decoder gradients from dt_emb eliminates CIVO, confirming the gradient pathway analysis\"\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires Full-SGS to show meaningful intervention effect. Since dt_emb receives 93% of its gradient from observation decoders and 7% from KL dynamics, detaching the observation pathway leaves only the weak KL signal. This should dramatically reduce the embedding's ability to learn precise dt distinctions, preventing the CIVO mechanism. The nve stays below 1.5x (predicted 1.25x peak) because the residual 7% KL gradient is insufficient to create the precise dt conditioning that enables value exploitation. Performance is lower than baseline (350 vs 916) because the intervention also impairs the world model's dt-awareness for state prediction, similar to 008f's reward degradation but through a different mechanism. The key finding: this experimentally confirms that the observation decoder→dt_emb gradient pathway is the causal enabler of CIVO.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Gradient intervention comparison\"\n        columns: [\"config\", \"dt_emb_grad_fraction_removed\", \"nve_peak\", \"collapse\", \"eval_105K\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"008d (no intervention)\", \"0%\", \"4.12x\", \"yes\", 131]\n          - [\"016e (reward detach = no-op)\", \"0% (reward grad = 0)\", \"10.42x\", \"yes\", 32]\n          - [\"018 (obs decoder detach)\", \"93%\", \"1.25x\", \"no\", 350]\n          - [\"008f (dt_emb absent)\", \"100%\", \"1.02x\", \"no\", 503]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Gradient intervention dose-response: x-axis = fraction of dt_emb gradient removed, y-axis = peak NVE. Clear threshold between 0% (CIVO) and 93% (no CIVO).\"\n    analysis: \"Full-SGS experimentally validates the gradient decomposition analysis from D061. The 93% observation decoder gradient is both necessary and (nearly) sufficient for CIVO. Removing it reduces NVE from pathological (>4x) to healthy (1.25x). This is the mechanistic smoking gun: the dt_embedding learns precise temporal distinctions through observation reconstruction, and this precision is what enables the critic exploitation feedback loop.\"\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"c5c7d6db-7a89-4828-b637-4d0ede508a4e","total_cost_usd":0.7924910000000001,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":40578,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":19892,"server_tool_use":